00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:42.976
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.150
KEYSER: Good morning, everyone.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:55.850
Welcome to MIT's 2003rd
commencement exercises

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.790
for the year 2003 in
historically Killian Court.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:02.270
That's not 2003 commencements,
that's the year.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.760
But it's early for me.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.860
In approximately
90 minutes, we'll

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.980
be joined by our
guests of honor,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:13.130
who you'll be able to see
on the big screen over there

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:16.070
to your right.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.300
That's that great big jumbotron.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.700
And you will be able to
watch the commencement

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:28.400
as our guests walk around and
then come up into the court.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.000
We will visit with a few
of them in a few minutes.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.690
But right now, we have
prepared something

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:40.280
to help pass the time
pleasantly for you.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.460
But before we do that,
I have an announcement

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:43.330
that I have to read.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:47.010
So please bear with me.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:50.580
I need to inform you of the
following safety procedure.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.230
In the event of a
sudden emergency,

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.180
such as a severe
thunderstorm that

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.630
would necessitate
evacuation of this Court,

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:59.820
you will hear an announcement.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:03.330
Emergency exit signs would
be immediately posted,

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.940
indicating routes you
should take to leave.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.310
These signs will lead
you to a corridor

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.440
where MIT personnel
will further direct

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.375
your travel to a safe location.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:16.800
Now, this is a glorious day.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.600
And that's MIT commencement
speak for "it's not raining."

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.030
And it's supposed to get better.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:27.450
So let us count our blessings.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:31.620
Welcome to MIT's Killian Court.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.680
This setting is a
very rich one that

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.840
plays an important
role in MIT's history,

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.870
and I would like to introduce
to you my colleague, Warren

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:44.130
Seamans, MIT Historian and
Former Director of the MIT

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:48.490
Museum, who knows more about
MIT lore than perhaps anyone.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:48.990
Warren?

00:06:52.469 --> 00:06:58.550
SEAMANS: This is the 87th
season for Killian Court.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:05.840
In 1916, MIT moved from its
home of 55 years in the Copley

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.540
Square of Boston's
Back Bay to this site.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.280
And this, since that time,
has been literally the center

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.260
of MIT activities.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:18.440
For the first several years,
graduations were held--

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:20.990
or commencements were
held here in the court.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:27.080
However, in 1922 the tent that
was housing them blew down.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:31.340
So henceforth, after that,
for the next approximately 50

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.590
years, there were other
sites found for it,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.680
including Symphony
Hall in Boston

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:42.820
and the Rockwell Cage, which
is the large athletic center.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:46.940
However, about 20 years ago
they moved back to this area,

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.310
and so we are here today.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:53.255
To expound a little bit on
the historic part of this--

00:07:53.255 --> 00:07:56.870
this has been the home of
everything from inaugurations

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:02.600
of new presidents to the
ceremonies commemorating

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:05.060
the passing of
former presidents.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:10.210
It was originally known
as the Great Court,

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.070
and it comprised three parts--

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:15.810
Lowell and Dupont
Courts-- because they're

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:18.200
the people who actually
paid for the land

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.500
that MIT was built on in 1916.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:28.790
In 1974, it was renamed the
James Rhyne Killian, Jr. Court,

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:32.179
in honor of James Rhyne
Killian who, at that point,

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:34.640
had been both President
and Chairman of the MIT

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.320
Corporation.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.659
It has also served--
this locale has

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:41.900
served as the final
resting place for at least

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:43.490
two presidents whose
ashes have been

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:46.100
spread throughout the Court.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:52.190
So you are really treading on
some of MIT's most holy ground.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.625
With that, I will
turn back to Jay.

00:08:54.625 --> 00:08:57.280
KEYSER: Thank you, Warren.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:01.000
So Killian Court is kind
of like MIT's Central Park.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.540
All kinds of things go on here--
picnics, research, gatherings

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:05.140
like this.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:07.190
And to give you a
better idea of that,

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.090
we are going to offer a bird's
eye view of this setting

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:14.360
through this video report on
MIT's robo-copter project.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.634
So please, direct your
eyes to the humongous-tron.

00:09:18.634 --> 00:09:21.004
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:09:24.322 --> 00:09:31.086
- This project is to demonstrate
automated acrobatic maneuvering

00:09:31.086 --> 00:09:32.700
for an aerial vehicle.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:38.330
- In the Killian Court, we
have done a flight in so-called

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.510
Control Augmentation System,
where the pilot gives very

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:42.410
high-level commands.

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:47.975
For example, go forward
or go sideways or turn,

00:09:47.975 --> 00:09:49.952
go slightly higher,
slightly lower.

00:09:49.952 --> 00:09:51.410
The pilot doesn't
have to take care

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.000
of stabilization of the system.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.905
That's done by automatic
control system.

00:09:54.905 --> 00:09:56.720
- The vehicle we
did not work on.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.810
We purchased a vehicle directly
from a miniature aircraft.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:02.847
- Nice.

00:10:02.847 --> 00:10:05.400
- We picked this machine
because it's very cheap.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.270
It's mass manufactured.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:12.030
And we could outfit it with our
own electronics very quickly.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.390
- This is our ground station.

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:17.380
It's a very old laptop,
about three years old.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:20.280
It runs a real-time
operating system.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:26.390
And it gets the data from the
helicopter about the sensors

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:30.440
and the state, [INAUDIBLE]
velocities, altitude,

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:32.000
and everything else.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.820
The ground station
operator monitors

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.549
that all of the
sensors are doing fine.

00:10:36.549 --> 00:10:37.840
And then we take all this data.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.000
We analyze it, we
record the trajectories

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.410
of acrobatic maneuvers the
[INAUDIBLE] has performed.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:49.400
And we designed the controllers
that follow this trajectory.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:54.500
- So what happens is we use
filtering technologies in order

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:59.930
to synthesize this information
into a complete and accurate

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:04.700
estimate of where the helicopter
is and what it's doing.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:12.990
- Previously a small rotocraft
and large rotocraft as well,

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.020
when they were
flown autonomously,

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.300
have been flying very
non-aggressive trajectories.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:24.720
And what we have contributed
is autonomous aggressive

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:25.730
Maneuvering.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:29.420
So we were able to
design the control laws

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:33.500
that allow us to perform an
aerobatic maneuver completely

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:34.070
hands off.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.950
- The applications could
include news gathering,

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:45.470
so gathering video footage
of hard-to-explore areas,

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:50.580
top of mountains, top of
volcanoes, fire situations,

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:58.900
any environment which is very
complicated, very contrived,

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:02.620
and that would require very
agile maneuvers and very agile

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.545
machines to operate.

00:12:04.545 --> 00:12:07.360
- You can also use it
for a number of military

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:10.960
applications-- for example,
urban surveillance and pursuit.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.790
- There's an aesthetic
component to our research,

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:18.370
which is to make such machines
as graceful as possible.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.800
What we would want is that
this machine eventually

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.359
behaves and flies as well,
if not better, than a bird.

00:12:25.359 --> 00:12:28.852
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:14:04.161 --> 00:14:05.670
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:09.300
KEYSER: MIT's mission
statement includes the phrase

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.300
"We seek to develop in each
member of the MIT community

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.750
the ability and passion to
work wisely, creatively,

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:19.290
and effectively for the
betterment of mankind."

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.140
The Nepal Water Project
illustrates this commitment

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:26.530
in action, and we'd like to
show you that on the jumbotron.

00:14:34.724 --> 00:14:37.134
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:14:39.062 --> 00:14:44.760
- The beginning of the Nepal
Water Project was in 1998.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:48.150
I was invited as a water
expert to a conference

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.120
called the Second International
Women on the Water Conference.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:54.900
There were some 75
Nepali peasant women

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:58.500
who had walked two and three
days from remote parts of Nepal

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:00.270
to attend this conference.

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:04.810
About 70% of people in Nepal
lack access to clean water.

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:10.050
So when a woman goes out and
collects water from the source,

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.990
chances are that water
will be contaminated.

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:15.540
Moreover, one in 10
children in Nepal

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.715
are dying from
waterborne diseases.

00:15:17.715 --> 00:15:20.880
- The most grave or sort
of pressing problems

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.520
in the world today,
in my opinion,

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:27.120
is diseases caused by
waterborne pathogens.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.390
So you have the leading cause
of childhood death in Nepal,

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:34.470
for example, and in many other
developing countries is either

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:38.070
from a disease caused
by pathogens in water

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:41.010
or a result of malnutrition.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:42.570
The systems that
we're all looking at

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:46.680
are point of use water filters,
and the idea behind that

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:49.020
is that it's an interim way--

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.690
because larger
distribution systems

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.490
won't be accessible
in the near term.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:57.480
- When I attended this
conference in 1998

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:00.240
and actually met the women
whose lives were impacted

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:03.692
by the water pollution problems
and they looked me in the eye

00:16:03.692 --> 00:16:06.150
and I looked them in the eye
and they said, my children are

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.210
sick and dying and you
are a water engineer who

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.750
knows the answers, tell us
how to solve this problem,

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.760
I felt a big
responsibility to come up

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.555
with some solutions that
would address that problem.

00:16:18.555 --> 00:16:21.720
- Well, the Nepal project
has been a great project.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:24.720
Susan Murcott has done
this for three years now.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:29.820
Many of our competing
programs, they only

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.590
require students
to take courses.

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:35.100
And we require them to
take courses and also do

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.210
a rigorous group project.

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:40.297
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:40.297 --> 00:16:45.480
- When they look for post-grad
studies, graduate studies,

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.704
I was specifically looking
for something applied

00:16:48.704 --> 00:16:49.870
in the developing countries.

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:53.280
I read on the web that
MIT, and specifically

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.740
the Master of Engineering,
was offering this program.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.050
And I specifically
came for this.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:01.140
- The Nepal Project
is now a good example

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:03.840
for students to come together
with interdisciplinary

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:06.500
backgrounds and work
on a real problem

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:09.810
and at the same time experience
some of the rest of the world

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:12.109
that many students
don't get a chance

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:15.390
to visit and to share that
experience with other students

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:16.200
in the program.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:18.033
It's a good opportunity
to work on something

00:17:18.033 --> 00:17:21.119
that's real world and
important to real people.

00:17:21.119 --> 00:17:23.790
- Doing [INAUDIBLE] studies on
water treatment for developing

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:25.550
countries is a
totally new thing,

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:28.674
which I probably would not get
a chance to do anywhere else.

00:17:28.674 --> 00:17:30.090
The unique part
about this project

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:33.840
is actually the ability
for every one of us

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.230
to get down to the field,
to the project country,

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.220
and actually do something and
make a difference to the people

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:40.720
there.

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:43.080
- Choosing a project
to do for my thesis,

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:47.640
I wanted to do something that
would impact people directly

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:48.870
and to help them.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:53.790
And the water and sanitation
projects that Susan

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.680
proposed all fit this bill.

00:17:56.680 --> 00:18:00.080
Arsenic was found
by an MEng student

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:02.959
about two years ago
in Nepal, and they

00:18:02.959 --> 00:18:04.500
suspected that there
would be arsenic

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:07.830
in Nepal because there's high
arsenic in West Bengal, India,

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:09.090
and in Bangladesh.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:11.910
Arsenic is naturally occurring
in the ground water there.

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:16.200
And this year we're
tackling three technologies,

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:17.820
and I'm doing an
iron-coated sand

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:19.910
technology that
will remove arsenic

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:23.000
when the water is passed by.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.910
- My arsenic filter
works excellent.

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.470
It removes 100% of
all the arsenic,

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:29.595
and I was really impressed.

00:18:29.595 --> 00:18:32.010
And I also did some
arsenic species and tests.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:37.885
And I visited over 50 different
wells and took samples,

00:18:37.885 --> 00:18:40.445
and it was personally
very successful.

00:18:40.445 --> 00:18:43.440
- Even though MIT
is famous as being

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.840
the pinnacle of high
technology, it's

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:49.920
also a pinnacle of
entrepreneurial and creativity

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.600
and innovation and
problem solving.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:56.610
And we are using the
best and brightest

00:18:56.610 --> 00:19:01.350
to focus their attention on
one of the biggest problems.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.660
I think of it as being
something like an Outward Bound

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:08.074
expedition for these kids,
only on a scientific level.

00:19:08.074 --> 00:19:10.410
- That is one of the very
important reasons why

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.672
I choose this program
instead of the other offer

00:19:12.672 --> 00:19:14.130
that I got from
other universities,

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:17.520
is I have the chance to
work in a developing country

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:19.155
and actually do something good.

00:19:19.155 --> 00:19:20.550
This kind of
program can actually

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:24.390
help to promote women's
role in society,

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:29.040
because they trained women
to be the well mechanics.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:32.400
So when they gain
this kind of position,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:35.940
they can gain more
respect from the society.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:39.380
- I think this project
really is my MIT experience.

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:42.440
I think that course work and
the other things I'm doing,

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.060
it helps with this
project, but I

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.730
think that this is the
reason I came to the program.

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:48.980
And this project is--

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:51.630
I'm hoping to take as much as
I can away from this project,

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:54.440
and I think that it's really
the centerpiece of my MIT

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:55.580
experience.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:59.720
- Kids come back from
these experiences changed--

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:02.870
not just changed as scientists,
but changed as human beings.

00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:05.300
And I've seen this time and
again, and it's very exciting.

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:08.060
- See-- students from all around
whom are attracted to this

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:11.240
project and interested in these
things is there's a genuine

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:15.830
desire to make a difference, not
through small steps and science

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:18.280
some time off in
the future but now.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:20.090
You know, these are
pressing problems

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:21.855
that need to be solved now.

00:20:21.855 --> 00:20:24.630
- I have this
poster from UNICEF.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:28.230
It says, "What do you want
to be when you grow up?"

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:30.130
And there's a
picture of a child.

00:20:30.130 --> 00:20:34.490
And the answer below is
"alive," exclamation point.

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.064
That's basically to tie it
back to the Women in Water

00:20:37.064 --> 00:20:38.480
conference-- what
these women were

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:41.752
asking for is, how do I
take care of my children?

00:20:41.752 --> 00:20:44.232
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:50.960
KEYSER: We're going to
use, through the magic

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:54.470
of technology, we're going to
go over to the Howard Johnson

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:58.310
Athletic Center and interview
one of the students there.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:02.720
It occurs to me that I didn't
tell you I'm an MIT Professor.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:04.940
That's why it's important
that you pay attention

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.830
to what I say.

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:09.765
Consider this free tuition.

00:21:09.765 --> 00:21:12.140
So now we're going to go over
to Howard Johnson's and see

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:13.280
if there's anybody for us.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:18.220
Hi, can you hear me?

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:19.900
BENIFLAH: I can hear
you loud and clear.

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:20.941
KEYSER: What's your name?

00:21:20.941 --> 00:21:22.420
BENIFLAH: Jacob Beniflah.

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:25.135
KEYSER: Jacob, what--
say your name again.

00:21:25.135 --> 00:21:26.865
BENIFLAH: Jacob Beniflah.

00:21:26.865 --> 00:21:27.490
KEYSER: Uh-huh.

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.440
And what school are you in?

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:30.772
BENIFLAH: School of Science.

00:21:30.772 --> 00:21:32.980
KEYSER: And you're getting
your degree in what today?

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:35.262
BENIFLAH: Brain and
cognitive science.

00:21:35.262 --> 00:21:36.595
KEYSER: Hey, that's what I'm in.

00:21:36.595 --> 00:21:38.200
BENIFLAH: Awesome.

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:40.064
KEYSER: Tell me, do
you have family here?

00:21:40.064 --> 00:21:41.730
BENIFLAH: Yeah, I
have-- both my parents

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:43.932
have been there since
7:20 in the morning.

00:21:43.932 --> 00:21:44.890
KEYSER: Where are they?

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:46.210
Where are Jacob's parents?

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:48.370
Raise your hand, please.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:49.315
Are they here?

00:21:49.315 --> 00:21:51.150
BENIFLAH: They should
be right at the front.

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:54.370
KEYSER: Stand up
and raise your head.

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:55.480
Everybody's standing up.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:57.820
How many parents
do you have, Jacob/

00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:58.826
BENIFLAH: I don't know.

00:21:58.826 --> 00:21:59.950
KEYSER: Oh, there they are.

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:00.670
Hi, everybody.

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:02.767
Say hello to them.

00:22:02.767 --> 00:22:03.850
BENIFLAH: Hi, Mom and Dad.

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:06.180
How are you doing?

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:07.680
KEYSER: We don't
have a mic on them,

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.100
but they say hello
to you, Jacob.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.350
Tell me, Jacob,
where did you live

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:13.630
when you lived here at MIT?

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:15.910
BENIFLAH: I lived at AEPi,
right across the river.

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:16.950
KEYSER: And how did you like it?

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:17.991
BENIFLAH: It was awesome.

00:22:17.991 --> 00:22:20.644
Best experience ever.

00:22:20.644 --> 00:22:22.060
KEYSER: But during
the winter when

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:24.520
you had to cross the
bridge, wasn't it cold?

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:28.575
BENIFLAH: I'm from Texas,
so I drive everywhere.

00:22:28.575 --> 00:22:32.200
KEYSER: [CHUCKLING]
What do you drive?

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:34.126
BENIFLAH: Jeep Grand Cherokee.

00:22:34.126 --> 00:22:35.500
KEYSER: I mean,
is it a big Jeep?

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:36.124
BENIFLAH: Yeah.

00:22:36.124 --> 00:22:36.680
Heated seats.

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:38.260
It's awesome.

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:40.420
It is the way to
cross the river.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:43.434
KEYSER: So tell me, Jacob,
have you paid for the car?

00:22:43.434 --> 00:22:45.235
BENIFLAH: Uh, my daddy.

00:22:45.235 --> 00:22:47.710
KEYSER: Ah, good.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:50.470
Daddy also paid for
the tuition, right?

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:51.070
BENIFLAH: Yes.

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:52.295
KEYSER: But he's from Texas.

00:22:52.295 --> 00:22:53.170
BENIFLAH: Yes, he is.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:54.280
Originally from Spain, though.

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:55.571
Both my parents are from Spain.

00:22:55.571 --> 00:22:56.830
KEYSER: Ah.

00:22:56.830 --> 00:22:57.670
Buenas dias.

00:22:57.670 --> 00:23:00.320
BENIFLAH: Buenas dias.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:02.620
KEYSER: [SPANISH].

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:05.530
I just said, I can
speak a little Spanish.

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:09.790
Tell me, Jacob, what are you
going to do when you graduate?

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:13.042
BENIFLAH: I'm going
straight to medical school.

00:23:13.042 --> 00:23:14.625
KEYSER: And you're
going to become a--

00:23:14.625 --> 00:23:16.900
do you know what medical
school you're going to?

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:18.970
BENIFLAH: UT
Southwestern in Dallas.

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:20.950
KEYSER: And have
you picked what sort

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:23.340
of a medical career you want?

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:24.610
BENIFLAH: I have no clue.

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:25.720
Some kind of doctor.

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:27.430
I don't know.

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:28.210
KEYSER: All right.

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:29.043
Thanks a lot, Jacob.

00:23:29.043 --> 00:23:30.850
And I see you have
some friends with you.

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:31.570
BENIFLAH: Yeah.

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:32.740
They want to say hi
to their parents.

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:33.282
Can they?

00:23:33.282 --> 00:23:34.240
KEYSER: Yes, of course.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:35.073
BENIFLAH: All right.

00:23:35.073 --> 00:23:36.820
KEYSER: We'll take them all.

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:38.230
Okay, what's your name?

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:39.380
STUDENT: Hi, Mom.

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:40.155
Hi, Dad.

00:23:40.155 --> 00:23:41.530
KEYSER: No, that's
not your name.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:42.904
STUDENT: Oh, I'm
Naomi Schmelzer.

00:23:42.904 --> 00:23:44.129
And I'm majoring in Course 9.

00:23:44.129 --> 00:23:45.670
KEYSER: Wait a
minute, wait a minute.

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:45.810
These people--

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:47.101
KURNIK: Hi, I'm Rebecca Kurnik.

00:23:47.101 --> 00:23:47.980
I'm a biology major.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:48.480
Hi!

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.680
KEYSER: You see, that's
an MIT student for you.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:53.242
They don't listen
to their professors.

00:23:53.242 --> 00:23:54.530
STUDENT: Hi, I'm Stephanie.

00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:55.715
I'm majoring in Course 3.

00:23:55.715 --> 00:23:57.670
And I just wanted to
say hi to my mom and dad

00:23:57.670 --> 00:23:59.431
and the rest of my family.

00:23:59.431 --> 00:23:59.930
BERGER: Ah.

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:01.300
Okay, you're Stephanie who?

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:01.800
What?

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:03.464
BENIFLAH: Stephanie Berger.

00:24:03.464 --> 00:24:04.630
KEYSER: Where's the Bergers?

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:06.682
Are the Bergers here?

00:24:06.682 --> 00:24:08.015
BENIFLAH: Are your parents here?

00:24:08.015 --> 00:24:09.171
BERGER: Yes.

00:24:09.171 --> 00:24:10.420
BENIFLAH: They should be here.

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:11.700
KEYSER: Raise your hand.

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.360
Would the Bergers
raise their hand?

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:14.540
Oh, there you are.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:15.040
Good.

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:15.915
Now, let's have the--

00:24:15.915 --> 00:24:17.080
and who's the other people?

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:18.910
You talked so fast,
I couldn't catch up.

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:20.140
What's your other names?

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:22.029
BENIFLAH: This is
Rebecca Kurnik.

00:24:22.029 --> 00:24:22.570
KEYSER: Okay.

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:23.737
Where is your parents?

00:24:23.737 --> 00:24:24.820
Where's Rebecca's parents?

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:25.900
BENIFLAH: Rebecca,
are your parents here?

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:27.160
KURNIK: Yeah, they should be.

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:28.840
I told my dad to
get up really early.

00:24:31.201 --> 00:24:32.950
KEYSER: So tell me,
Rebecca, where are you

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:35.926
going after you graduate?

00:24:35.926 --> 00:24:37.900
KURNIK: I'm also going
to medical school.

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:39.316
I'm going to be
in Boston, though.

00:24:39.316 --> 00:24:40.455
I'm going to Tufts.

00:24:40.455 --> 00:24:40.870
KEYSER: And what kind of--

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:41.703
KURNIK: I'm excited.

00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:47.340
BENIFLAH: What kind of
doctor do you want to be?

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:48.964
KURNIK: I don't know
what I want to be.

00:24:48.964 --> 00:24:49.740
We'll see.

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:51.495
Right now I'm just
going to graduate.

00:24:51.495 --> 00:24:52.299
KEYSER: Oh, great.

00:24:52.299 --> 00:24:54.090
Well, look, I think
it's really interesting

00:24:54.090 --> 00:24:55.950
that of the four that we've
talked to, two of them

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:56.850
want to be doctors.

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:58.314
BENIFLAH: Not two, but three.

00:24:58.314 --> 00:24:59.730
KEYSER: You mean
there's a third--

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:01.350
KURNIK: Naomi's also
going to medical school.

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:02.400
STUDENT: I'm going to Mt.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:04.855
Sinai next year for an MD/MPH.

00:25:04.855 --> 00:25:06.720
KEYSER: Well, I'll be darned.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:08.140
Three doctors.

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:09.990
And I was talking to
one of the gentlemen

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:12.720
here in the audience
who tells me that he

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.060
lives next door to a hospital.

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:18.220
So there must be a real
affinity between MIT students

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:20.180
and medicine.

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:22.120
BENIFLAH: There definitely is.

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.940
There definitely has to be.

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:26.050
KEYSER: All right.

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:27.730
Hey, listen guys,
we'll be seeing you

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:28.700
over here in a minute.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:29.980
But it's been great
talking to you.

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:31.060
BENIFLAH: Nice
talking to you too.

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:31.700
KEYSER: Nice talking to you.

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:32.658
And we'll see you soon.

00:25:32.658 --> 00:25:33.410
KURNIK: Thank you.

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:33.940
KEYSER: Okay.

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:34.648
BENIFLAH: Thanks.

00:25:34.648 --> 00:25:36.280
BERGER: Bye.

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:40.104
KEYSER: I feel as if America's
health is in good hands.

00:25:40.104 --> 00:25:42.520
I'm interested in learning a
little bit about the audience

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:43.020
here.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.010
For example, is
there anybody here

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:48.520
for whom this is not
your first commencement?

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:51.010
Have you been here
more than once?

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:53.680
If you've been here more than
once, come up here, will you?

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:57.520
If this is your second or third
commencement, come up here.

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:00.235
And I'm also interested in
who came here from the--

00:26:00.235 --> 00:26:02.779
who came from the
farthest distance?

00:26:02.779 --> 00:26:04.195
I want to see who
wins that prize.

00:26:06.731 --> 00:26:08.980
So if you think that you've
come here from the longest

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:10.270
distance-- we're
going to roll a tape,

00:26:10.270 --> 00:26:12.190
and while we're rolling the
tape, you guys come up here

00:26:12.190 --> 00:26:12.815
and we'll talk.

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:17.630
Okay.

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:21.530
MIT is world-renowned
for its excellence

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:24.590
in science and engineering
education and research.

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:27.260
What is less known
is that MIT also

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:30.500
has a thriving arts program.

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:34.351
Look at the huge-atron
over my shoulder.

00:26:34.351 --> 00:26:35.842
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:26:35.842 --> 00:26:39.321
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:27:13.117 --> 00:27:17.770
- One morning I came up the
steps of Massachusetts Avenue

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:24.240
and walked through the
door, and the entire space

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:27.660
had been transformed
into a wheat field.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:30.060
Innovative visual arts projects
like that are happening

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:33.150
all the time on campus.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:39.200
The arts at MIT, like science
and engineering at MIT,

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:44.490
are on the cutting edge
of their disciplines.

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:47.920
They are serving the students--

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:50.445
- And I've got a rap sheet
that's got a list of--

00:27:50.445 --> 00:27:53.160
- --and in the way they're
serving the students,

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.737
ultimately they're going to
be serving the entire society.

00:27:58.419 --> 00:28:02.017
- At MIT, you'll find some
of the best musicians.

00:28:02.017 --> 00:28:03.600
Virtuosos-- it could
be the person who

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:05.960
lives next door to
me has perfect pitch,

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:08.560
and if you name any song
whatsoever he can strum it down

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:09.970
out his guitar.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:11.650
If it has multiple
lines of music,

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:13.870
he can strum out all
lines on his guitar.

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:14.950
And you can see that.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:18.490
It's indicative of the level
of quality of the MIT Symphony

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:21.830
Orchestra, the MIT
Concert Choir, et cetera.

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:23.770
- Last year I got
to premiere a song

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:26.260
cycle written by
an MIT composer,

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:27.820
and that was a lot of fun.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:30.160
Modern poetry, modern
music, and everything.

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:32.424
- Everyone at MIT
cares about something,

00:28:32.424 --> 00:28:34.090
whether it's their
classes or their UROP

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:35.900
or some student
activity they do,

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:38.108
there's always something
that they really care about.

00:28:38.108 --> 00:28:41.230
- The thing about MIT students
that I most appreciate

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:46.030
is the elegance of their
thinking and the fact

00:28:46.030 --> 00:28:48.225
that it's sort of live
and let live here.

00:28:48.225 --> 00:28:52.105
- [SINGING "BLUE MOON" WITH NON
 ENGLISH LYRICS]

00:28:57.440 --> 00:29:00.060
- There's a point
of concentration

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.630
that any discipline requires,
whether it's engineering

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:06.850
or it's directing or
acting or painting.

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:10.620
That point of concentration
is the focal point.

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:14.100
It is the thing around which
you organize your thinking

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:16.140
and your technique.

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:19.080
And so the fact that there
is ensemble work where people

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:22.020
learn to work
collaboratively is something

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:25.920
that I see that people take
into their labs in a way

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:28.814
that you would hope that
labs work collaboratively.

00:29:28.814 --> 00:29:32.272
[MUSIC - SANTANA, "SMOOTH"]

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.475
- [LAUGHING]

00:29:55.465 --> 00:29:56.950
- I'll kill you!

00:29:56.950 --> 00:29:59.920
- Ooh, he kills me!

00:29:59.920 --> 00:30:02.600
- Well, right now
it's sort of the--

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:05.150
theatre is a way I can
investigate my own sort

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:07.400
of identity, especially
my own cultural identity.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:11.212
But I also enjoy theater because
it's such a collaborative art.

00:30:11.212 --> 00:30:13.670
And it's a lot like engineering,
which is one of my majors,

00:30:13.670 --> 00:30:16.185
and I also intend to
pursue engineering

00:30:16.185 --> 00:30:18.140
in that it is so
collaborative, and so you

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:20.389
get a lot of different
people's perspective on things.

00:30:24.227 --> 00:30:25.750
- I'm Arning.

00:30:25.750 --> 00:30:28.420
I'm the Curator at the MIT
List Visual Art Center.

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:32.290
And I'm here in our annual
Student Loan Exhibition.

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.780
MIT, quite usually,
has a collection

00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:39.680
of artworks that students
get to live with for a year.

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:43.300
- Arts are in every
new building now.

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:47.110
It's pervasive from
that point of view,

00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:49.630
and it's pervasive
from the things

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:51.970
that you see people
doing all over.

00:30:51.970 --> 00:30:54.450
My favorite program is
the Council Scholars,

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:58.410
of course, because I'm
very involved with them.

00:30:58.410 --> 00:31:03.820
It started with 20 or so
undergraduate students,

00:31:03.820 --> 00:31:06.040
and now we have both
undergraduate and graduate

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:07.330
students.

00:31:07.330 --> 00:31:10.450
Computer scientists,
they're chemical engineers,

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:14.650
they're architectural
students, and they

00:31:14.650 --> 00:31:18.790
are not, with a few exceptions,
actually going out and leading

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:20.740
professional lives as artists.

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:23.260
But this is something that's
been very important to them

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:24.160
all their lives.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:29.410
- We find our students,
a certain number of them,

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:32.740
go on to graduate study
in music and in the arts.

00:31:32.740 --> 00:31:35.770
Some of them go on to become
leaders of arts organizations

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:37.280
within their communities.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:39.820
Some of them go on to
sing in organizations

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:43.780
or performing organizations like
the Tanglewood Festival Chorus.

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:46.960
And in a couple of
notable situations,

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:50.190
I have myself a
number of times I've

00:31:50.190 --> 00:31:53.140
been engaged by organizations
whose presidents were

00:31:53.140 --> 00:31:57.370
either in my class
at MIT 20 years ago

00:31:57.370 --> 00:32:00.166
or had been at MIT
years before that.

00:32:00.166 --> 00:32:03.082
[CHAMBER MUSIC]

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:18.710
My first visit to MIT, when
I walked through the halls,

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:20.940
I remember passing through
the math department

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:24.030
on the way to Building
14, where we used to be.

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:29.460
And I looked in a door and
saw mathematicians hunched

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:34.004
in carrels, and I felt right at
home, because it looked exactly

00:32:34.004 --> 00:32:35.670
like walking down the
halls of Juilliard

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:38.970
into the practice rooms
and seeing people working

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:40.920
personally on something.

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:44.070
- One of the amazing
things about MIT students

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:47.580
is the incredible wide range
of interests and abilities

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:51.090
that extends far beyond the
intellectual and the academic.

00:32:51.090 --> 00:32:54.720
And our special guest with us
this section of the concert

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:56.340
is no exception to that.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:58.586
His name is Jonathan Lee.

00:32:58.586 --> 00:33:00.065
