00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:47.570
I don't know if you know this
or not, but you can tell--

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:51.140
you can tell what the
temperature is outside

00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:53.060
if you can hear a cricket.

00:40:53.060 --> 00:40:57.760
What you do is you count the
number of chirps in 15 seconds,

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:03.020
add 40, and that's the
temperature outside.

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:05.577
So now what I'm going
to do is, I'm going

00:41:05.577 --> 00:41:06.660
to give you a little test.

00:41:06.660 --> 00:41:08.190
[CHIRPS]

00:41:08.190 --> 00:41:11.700
Okay, what's the temperature?

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:13.864
Good.

00:41:13.864 --> 00:41:15.530
In the next video,
you're going to learn

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:18.440
about research that
also uses crickets,

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:20.420
but they aren't insects.

00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:22.280
The Cricket Indoor
Location System

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:24.110
is an example of a
research initiative

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:26.810
within the School of
Engineering that focuses

00:41:26.810 --> 00:41:29.750
on information engineering.

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:33.280
These crickets are
part of Project Oxygen,

00:41:33.280 --> 00:41:35.330
a partnership among the
laboratory for computer

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:37.670
science, the Artificial
Intelligence Lab,

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:39.620
and several major
corporations that

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:41.870
seek to make computation
and communication as

00:41:41.870 --> 00:41:45.590
abundant and natural to
use as oxygen in the air.

00:41:45.590 --> 00:41:48.404
Now let's look at the Cricket
Indoor Location System.

00:41:48.404 --> 00:41:49.070
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:41:49.070 --> 00:41:52.040
- Oxygen is a large project
between the labs, computer

00:41:52.040 --> 00:41:55.040
science, and the Artificial
Intelligence Lab at MIT.

00:41:55.040 --> 00:41:58.850
And it's trying to
build systems in a world

00:41:58.850 --> 00:42:02.850
where computing is plentiful
and communication is pervasive.

00:42:02.850 --> 00:42:04.610
So today we have
computers on our desks,

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:06.200
but in the Oxygen
vision, you have

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:08.660
computers everywhere
around us-- in the walls,

00:42:08.660 --> 00:42:11.330
in objects and
devices all around us.

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:13.430
And they're all
networked together.

00:42:13.430 --> 00:42:16.640
A critical piece of information
that you need is location.

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:18.102
You need to know
where objects are,

00:42:18.102 --> 00:42:19.560
you need to know
where devices are,

00:42:19.560 --> 00:42:21.350
and you need to know
where users are.

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:25.130
So Cricket is a technology that
provides location information

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:29.000
to users in indoor areas
and in urban areas.

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:32.150
So Cricket is intended for
indoor deployment-- places

00:42:32.150 --> 00:42:36.020
like campuses or museums or
shopping malls or subways

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:39.020
or airports, where
GPS doesn't work.

00:42:39.020 --> 00:42:42.650
The way Cricket works is there
are devices called beacons.

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:44.510
It has a radio
transmitter on it,

00:42:44.510 --> 00:42:47.330
and it has a couple of
other funny-looking things

00:42:47.330 --> 00:42:49.160
that are ultrasonic
transmitters.

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:52.640
And what Cricket beacons do
is they transmit information.

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:55.190
They're programmed
with information

00:42:55.190 --> 00:42:57.290
about their own location,
and they periodically

00:42:57.290 --> 00:43:00.410
transmit this information
in the form of chirps.

00:43:00.410 --> 00:43:02.450
Now, you have users
and other devices

00:43:02.450 --> 00:43:06.470
in the environment that have an
analogous piece of machinery,

00:43:06.470 --> 00:43:08.870
or a little device attached
to them, called listeners.

00:43:08.870 --> 00:43:10.580
And what the
listeners are doing is

00:43:10.580 --> 00:43:13.820
they're receiving the
information from these chirps,

00:43:13.820 --> 00:43:16.880
and they're doing some
calculations and computations

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:21.140
on these listener devices and
determining their location.

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:26.990
You often are asked as a
child how far away a cloud is.

00:43:26.990 --> 00:43:29.750
And the way you estimate
that is to first observe

00:43:29.750 --> 00:43:32.360
when you see the lightning
and then wait a few seconds

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:34.340
and you hear thunder
a little bit later.

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:36.555
And you use that time
difference to estimate how far

00:43:36.555 --> 00:43:38.180
away the cloud is
because sound travels

00:43:38.180 --> 00:43:39.710
so much slower than light.

00:43:39.710 --> 00:43:41.060
This is the same idea.

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:44.540
Our equivalent of lightning
is the radio frequency message

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:47.690
sent on a wireless channel,
and our equivalent of thunder

00:43:47.690 --> 00:43:48.890
is an ultrasonic message.

00:43:48.890 --> 00:43:49.690
It's ultrasound.

00:43:49.690 --> 00:43:52.310
You can't hear it, but it
travels at the speed of sound.

00:43:52.310 --> 00:43:55.460
And we use this time difference
of arrival technique.

00:43:55.460 --> 00:43:59.126
We started off doing things like
navigation application, which

00:43:59.126 --> 00:44:00.500
automatically
discovers where you

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:03.025
are, downloads maps, and shows
you where you want to go.

00:44:03.025 --> 00:44:04.400
You tell it where
you want to go,

00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:06.154
and then it gives
you directions.

00:44:06.154 --> 00:44:07.820
We've also worked on
entertainment-style

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:11.270
applications, where we
have live television coming

00:44:11.270 --> 00:44:12.344
onto handheld devices.

00:44:12.344 --> 00:44:14.510
You're watching television
on your handheld computer

00:44:14.510 --> 00:44:16.430
and you enter a room
with a bigger screen,

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:20.330
and with a single operation
you can cause that television

00:44:20.330 --> 00:44:22.730
stream to seamlessly
migrate to a bigger screen.

00:44:22.730 --> 00:44:25.700
We've also done
applications for hospitals

00:44:25.700 --> 00:44:29.350
where the idea is to use this
to keep track of patients

00:44:29.350 --> 00:44:30.850
and keep track of
medical equipment.

00:44:30.850 --> 00:44:34.640
We are now starting to
work with MIT facilities

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:36.420
to deploy sensor
networks, networks

00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:38.870
of little tiny sensors
attached with location that

00:44:38.870 --> 00:44:42.830
will keep track of smoke
and fire and chemicals

00:44:42.830 --> 00:44:44.120
and so forth for safety.

00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:45.860
We've also done some
fun applications

00:44:45.860 --> 00:44:47.960
like games, where
you're actually

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:50.709
in the real world moving
around, and you see your actions

00:44:50.709 --> 00:44:52.250
in the virtual world
of the computer.

00:44:52.250 --> 00:44:54.041
People have also talked
about using cricket

00:44:54.041 --> 00:44:56.150
in construction
systems, where you

00:44:56.150 --> 00:44:59.720
want to keep track of equipment,
lots of which are moving,

00:44:59.720 --> 00:45:01.880
and make sure that your
planning of construction

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:04.010
goes according to what
you had envisioned.

00:45:04.010 --> 00:45:05.737
So I think it's an
important project

00:45:05.737 --> 00:45:07.820
because it's interdisciplinary
and brings together

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:10.535
a lot of different disciplines
within information engineering.

00:45:10.535 --> 00:45:13.385
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:16.460
SEAMANS: For those
of you old enough

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:20.930
to remember the popular 1970s
television series The Six

00:45:20.930 --> 00:45:25.740
Million Dollar Man
and Bionic Woman,

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:29.240
you may think that some of the
new medical developments we

00:45:29.240 --> 00:45:33.380
hear about in the news are
reminiscent of bionics,

00:45:33.380 --> 00:45:36.380
those technological
advanced components that

00:45:36.380 --> 00:45:40.790
give our bodies superhuman
physical abilities although we

00:45:40.790 --> 00:45:43.730
haven't quite reached
