00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:48.590
we are building the foundation
for a new age of medical,

00:45:48.590 --> 00:45:52.580
environmental, and other
societal breakthroughs made

00:45:52.580 --> 00:45:57.710
possible by the melding of
modern biology and engineering.

00:45:57.710 --> 00:46:01.760
MIT's School of Engineering
is at the forefront

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:06.590
of this new field of
biological engineering.

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:12.500
The next video presents one
example of current research

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:17.270
in its biological engineering
initiative, the Liver Chip.

00:46:17.270 --> 00:46:19.543
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:46:20.042 --> 00:46:22.910
- I originally got started in
the field of tissue engineering

00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:26.720
by trying to replace developed
methodologies for replacing

00:46:26.720 --> 00:46:27.790
organs in people.

00:46:27.790 --> 00:46:31.550
So for example, ways to
take a single donor organ

00:46:31.550 --> 00:46:33.740
like liver and use
it in 100 patients

00:46:33.740 --> 00:46:35.990
so that you could take
cells out and grow them

00:46:35.990 --> 00:46:38.990
in little reactors
and transplant them

00:46:38.990 --> 00:46:40.340
into the patient.

00:46:40.340 --> 00:46:43.100
And then I had an epiphany
about five years ago,

00:46:43.100 --> 00:46:46.220
in that right now the
leading cause of transplants

00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:48.230
in the Western world
is hepatitis C.

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:51.410
And it's been very difficult to
develop drugs for hepatitis C--

00:46:51.410 --> 00:46:53.540
we've known about it
for over 20 years--

00:46:53.540 --> 00:46:56.060
because there's no way to
culture the virus in vitro.

00:46:56.060 --> 00:46:58.267
So liver cells
lose their ability

00:46:58.267 --> 00:47:00.350
to be infected when you
take them out and put them

00:47:00.350 --> 00:47:01.490
in culture.

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:04.310
So the epiphany was, maybe
what we should really

00:47:04.310 --> 00:47:07.280
be doing is building
physiological models of tissue

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:10.490
so we can understand
disease, because if it's me,

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:12.140
I'd rather have a
cure for the disease

00:47:12.140 --> 00:47:15.320
and never get an organ
transplant than ultimately have

00:47:15.320 --> 00:47:17.180
some organ grown in my lab.

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:20.250
So if we can head off and
prevent organ transplants,

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:22.075
it will be much
better for the patient

00:47:22.075 --> 00:47:24.200
than if we build an organ
to put in them after they

00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:25.580
get really, really sick.

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:27.800
So we're developing
a hepatitis model.

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:29.930
That's really the proof
that you have something

00:47:29.930 --> 00:47:31.130
that functions as liver.

00:47:31.130 --> 00:47:34.070
We still have a ways to go
to demonstrate definitively

00:47:34.070 --> 00:47:36.380
that we're getting the kind
of functional responses

00:47:36.380 --> 00:47:38.370
that you want to mimic
the in vivo liver.

00:47:38.370 --> 00:47:41.330
So we're not all there yet,
but we have every indication

00:47:41.330 --> 00:47:44.030
that we're headed very
much in the right direction

00:47:44.030 --> 00:47:46.370
compared to other
methods of keeping liver

00:47:46.370 --> 00:47:48.110
cells alive outside the body.

00:47:48.110 --> 00:47:51.800
What we're doing is using
micro-fabrication technology

00:47:51.800 --> 00:47:54.560
to build little
three-dimensional scaffolds

00:47:54.560 --> 00:47:58.940
that coax a mixture of cells out
of the liver to reform a liver

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:00.570
tissue and culture.

00:48:00.570 --> 00:48:02.870
So in liver, there are
a number of cell types

00:48:02.870 --> 00:48:04.250
that form the tissue.

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:06.110
And they're put
together in repeat units

00:48:06.110 --> 00:48:07.890
called a capillary bed.

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:09.740
And when the liver
needs more mass--

00:48:09.740 --> 00:48:13.080
so let's say you have a little
tumor taken out of the liver,

00:48:13.080 --> 00:48:13.820
it will regrow.

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:16.230
It adds a whole bunch
of these capillary beds.

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:18.890
So all we do is try to
rebuild that in culture

00:48:18.890 --> 00:48:22.940
by building a scaffold
that causes cells taken out

00:48:22.940 --> 00:48:24.800
of the liver to
grow into the shape

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:26.196
that they would in the body.

00:48:26.196 --> 00:48:28.070
Although we're currently
still using silicon,

00:48:28.070 --> 00:48:30.710
we're also moving into
polymers and other material

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:32.060
to build our scaffold.

00:48:32.060 --> 00:48:34.280
Everything is put
together in a bioreactor

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:36.320
and so we flow fluid
through the tissue,

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:38.540
just like blood would
flow through in the body.

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:42.470
And then we can look at it via
microscopy and measure things

00:48:42.470 --> 00:48:45.080
and understand what's
happening in the tissue.

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:47.229
There are an enormous
number of applications.

00:48:47.229 --> 00:48:48.770
In a way, almost
everything you would

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:51.260
like to do to human
tissue you could

00:48:51.260 --> 00:48:53.600
think of doing in this system.

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:55.430
Cell cultures only goes so far.

00:48:55.430 --> 00:48:57.260
We're trying to
capture the complexity

00:48:57.260 --> 00:48:59.990
of the cell-cell
interactions in human tissues

00:48:59.990 --> 00:49:03.349
and study a variety of diseases,
a variety of drug therapies,

00:49:03.349 --> 00:49:03.890
and so forth.

00:49:03.890 --> 00:49:05.900
It's really, I
think, a new frontier

00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:08.750
for how we study human disease.

00:49:08.750 --> 00:49:10.940
I can't imagine doing
this kind of research

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:12.800
at any other place than MIT.

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:14.930
MIT has really been
at the forefront of,

00:49:14.930 --> 00:49:17.450
how do you connect
modern biology,

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:21.650
forefront-leading biology, with
forefront-leading engineering?

00:49:21.650 --> 00:49:24.061
And MIT did this by
creating a special structure

00:49:24.061 --> 00:49:26.060
in the School of Engineering
called the Division

00:49:26.060 --> 00:49:27.506
of Biological Engineering.

00:49:27.506 --> 00:49:29.630
So some of the faculty in
the School of Engineering

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:32.690
have 50/50 appointments
in this division

00:49:32.690 --> 00:49:36.320
so we can all come together
around an interest in biology.

00:49:36.320 --> 00:49:39.650
Some of the biology faculty are
also appointed in the division,

00:49:39.650 --> 00:49:41.840
and we have a core
group of biologists

00:49:41.840 --> 00:49:43.590
who are 100% in the division.

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:46.100
So we have a continuum
from those engineers who

00:49:46.100 --> 00:49:47.570
don't know anything
about biology

00:49:47.570 --> 00:49:50.660
but are inventing really
cool technologies all the way

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:53.630
to the biologists who
know really all the things

00:49:53.630 --> 00:49:56.450
about stem cells but who
don't necessarily get involved

00:49:56.450 --> 00:49:57.590
in technology development.

00:49:57.590 --> 00:50:00.290
And the Division is a
way to do this and train

00:50:00.290 --> 00:50:02.570
a whole new breed of students
who will go out and push

00:50:02.570 --> 00:50:05.690
the frontier even further
by creating a new discipline

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:07.511
of biological engineering.

