01:05:12.460 --> 01:05:17.970
- We are at the dawn of
a true innovation age.

01:05:17.970 --> 01:05:22.320
It's estimated that the entire
store of human knowledge

01:05:22.320 --> 01:05:25.096
now doubles every five years.

01:05:25.096 --> 01:05:30.130
- I would like to thank MIT for
this incredible honor, which

01:05:30.130 --> 01:05:33.340
is, I have to say, the first
academic honor I've ever

01:05:33.340 --> 01:05:34.810
received.

01:05:34.810 --> 01:05:36.940
So it's nice to
start at the top.

01:05:36.940 --> 01:05:40.000
This is such a
great institution,

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:43.210
dedicated to the
search for the new

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:47.590
and to the openness of thought
that that search requires.

01:05:47.590 --> 01:05:52.030
- This case is is the
great test of where

01:05:52.030 --> 01:05:56.170
we stand on the issue of
freedom and solidarity

01:05:56.170 --> 01:05:58.787
and the future of
our own culture.

01:05:58.787 --> 01:06:02.560
- For in the end I believe
the real challenge of history

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:05.620
is to resist the tendencies
so prevalent today to label,

01:06:05.620 --> 01:06:08.920
to stereotype, to expose,
to denigrate, and instead

01:06:08.920 --> 01:06:12.020
to bring common sense and
empathy to our subjects

01:06:12.020 --> 01:06:14.620
so that the past can
truly come alive if only

01:06:14.620 --> 01:06:18.040
for a few moments in all of
its beauty, glory, sadness,

01:06:18.040 --> 01:06:19.760
and complexity.

01:06:19.760 --> 01:06:22.240
- Young people
like you are always

01:06:22.240 --> 01:06:24.790
the vanguard of any
social movement,

01:06:24.790 --> 01:06:28.540
setting an energetic tone
of courage and commitment.

01:06:28.540 --> 01:06:33.670
And now we need you
to once again lead us

01:06:33.670 --> 01:06:37.165
to a higher and
more noble destiny.

01:06:37.165 --> 01:06:41.950
- 21st century America
belongs to you.

01:06:41.950 --> 01:06:43.720
Take good care of it.

01:06:43.720 --> 01:06:45.160
Thank you, and god bless you.

01:06:54.622 --> 01:06:56.614
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:06:57.610 --> 01:06:58.970
KEYSER: Hello, everybody.

01:06:58.970 --> 01:07:00.740
I keep popping up
like a bad penny.

01:07:00.740 --> 01:07:02.000
I'm over here under the tent.

01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:03.490
Can you see me?

01:07:03.490 --> 01:07:06.170
I'm talking to
Larry Isaacson, who

01:07:06.170 --> 01:07:09.950
is Assistant Director of
the Music Division at Boston

01:07:09.950 --> 01:07:11.300
Conservatory.

01:07:11.300 --> 01:07:14.110
Larry has been affiliated
with MIT in the past.

01:07:14.110 --> 01:07:16.480
He was director of
the brass ensemble.

01:07:16.480 --> 01:07:22.730
And now Larry is conducting the
music for the procession today.

01:07:22.730 --> 01:07:26.310
I mean, no commencement would
be anywhere without music,

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:29.850
and we've got a terrific
conductor and ensemble here.

01:07:29.850 --> 01:07:30.920
mass Brass it is.

01:07:30.920 --> 01:07:32.590
Let me introduce Larry Issacson.

01:07:32.590 --> 01:07:33.340
ISAACSON: Hello.

01:07:33.340 --> 01:07:34.073
How are you?

01:07:34.073 --> 01:07:35.880
KEYSER: Larry, who
do we have here?

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:38.450
Do we have any musicians who
actually are also at MIT here?

01:07:38.450 --> 01:07:39.680
ISAACSON: We have several
musicians, actually,

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:40.670
who work here at MIT--

01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:42.665
Bob Marlatt, who's
in our horn section,

01:07:42.665 --> 01:07:44.250
Wes Hopper is in the
trombone section,

01:07:44.250 --> 01:07:46.004
and Mike Epstein used
to work here at MIT.

01:07:46.004 --> 01:07:48.170
KEYSER: And let's see, you
also have a group playing

01:07:48.170 --> 01:07:49.040
on the front steps?

01:07:49.040 --> 01:07:49.915
ISAACSON: Yes, we do.

01:07:49.915 --> 01:07:54.290
And actually an MIT grad is
on the steps of 77 Mass Ave.

01:07:54.290 --> 01:07:57.180
KEYSER: Now, Larry, I know
this is your fourth time here.

01:07:57.180 --> 01:07:59.480
And I know that
each time you like

01:07:59.480 --> 01:08:01.290
to do something a
little different.

01:08:01.290 --> 01:08:04.805
What have you done different for
us for the 2003 commencement?

01:08:04.805 --> 01:08:06.680
ISAACSON: There's two
things that we've done.

01:08:06.680 --> 01:08:09.350
We've added some Boston
Pops-style music,

01:08:09.350 --> 01:08:12.760
believe it or not, some
Leroy Anderson, some Strauss,

01:08:12.760 --> 01:08:15.710
"Tritsch-Trasch Polka," and the
professionals and recessionals,

01:08:15.710 --> 01:08:18.920
as well as a new
arrangement for Hartman--

01:08:18.920 --> 01:08:20.689
by Hartman, called
"Festival Sounds."

01:08:20.689 --> 01:08:22.430
And it's a wonderful
new processional

01:08:22.430 --> 01:08:23.750
that we're going to
be using this year,

01:08:23.750 --> 01:08:25.208
in addition to the
ceremonial march

01:08:25.208 --> 01:08:27.709
we had commissioned three
years ago by Kevin [INAUDIBLE]..

01:08:27.709 --> 01:08:29.395
KEYSER: That's terrific.

01:08:29.395 --> 01:08:30.520
Thank you very much, Larry.

01:08:30.520 --> 01:08:33.140
So I think, without
further ado, we're

01:08:33.140 --> 01:08:37.380
going to turn to the music and
wish you all a wonderful day.

01:08:37.380 --> 01:08:38.149
Thank you, Larry.

01:08:38.149 --> 01:08:39.190
ISAACSON: You're welcome.

01:10:55.184 --> 01:11:00.673
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:14:51.211 --> 01:14:56.201
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:17:47.857 --> 01:17:52.847
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:22:43.764 --> 01:22:48.255
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:26:30.809 --> 01:26:42.600
KEYSER: [NO AUDIO] --15 minutes.

01:26:45.369 --> 01:26:46.910
You'll be able to
tell the graduates.

01:26:46.910 --> 01:26:47.856
They'll be the people.

01:27:00.990 --> 01:27:04.280
Well, I've got some
good news, everyone.

01:27:04.280 --> 01:27:05.405
The procession is underway.

01:27:08.060 --> 01:27:11.403
We are on time and under budget.

01:27:15.750 --> 01:27:19.310
The procession will be
here in about 15 minutes,

01:27:19.310 --> 01:27:20.970
and it will come up
the center aisle.

01:27:20.970 --> 01:27:22.830
And you'll recognize
the graduates.

01:27:22.830 --> 01:27:24.910
They'll be the ones in
the long black gowns

01:27:24.910 --> 01:27:26.663
and the funny little hats.

01:27:30.030 --> 01:27:32.190
Now, for those of
you who are smoking,

01:27:32.190 --> 01:27:37.380
I wonder if I could ask you
to stop smoking at this point,

01:27:37.380 --> 01:27:39.900
both for your own safety
and for their safety

01:27:39.900 --> 01:27:43.400
and comfort of those
around you, for the rest

01:27:43.400 --> 01:27:44.930
of the commencement.

01:27:44.930 --> 01:27:48.180
Thank you very much, and
have a wonderful time.

01:27:48.180 --> 01:27:48.680
Bye-bye.

01:28:00.487 --> 01:28:05.976
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:31:15.596 --> 01:31:20.087
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:39:12.640 --> 01:39:17.630
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:42:35.733 --> 01:42:38.727
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:42:50.703 --> 01:42:52.200
KEYSER: [INAUDIBLE].

01:42:52.200 --> 01:42:58.188
And we are sitting in a vantage
point beneath the stage.

01:42:58.188 --> 01:43:01.854
But through the wonders
of modern technology,

01:43:01.854 --> 01:43:05.306
we've got a bird's eye view
of the entire processional.

01:43:05.306 --> 01:43:09.460
SEAMANS: Everything is
happening right in front of us.

01:43:09.460 --> 01:43:13.210
From our vantage point,
we see the three flags

01:43:13.210 --> 01:43:17.070
coming in under the guard
of the state police.

01:43:17.070 --> 01:43:22.510
This will soon drive across, up
to the podium where the flags

01:43:22.510 --> 01:43:23.908
will be planted in place.

01:43:26.710 --> 01:43:32.050
The view here, of course,
is the main dome of MIT,

01:43:32.050 --> 01:43:40.080
dedicated exactly 87
years ago on June 13.

01:43:40.080 --> 01:43:42.290
You will find, as
we go through this,

01:43:42.290 --> 01:43:44.780
we'll be talking a
lot about tradition.

01:43:44.780 --> 01:43:51.980
MIT is an institution that is
exceedingly rich in tradition.

01:43:51.980 --> 01:43:56.090
And we will try and pinpoint
a lot of those things

01:43:56.090 --> 01:43:57.905
as we go through.

01:43:57.905 --> 01:43:59.660
KEYSER: The facade
that you see up

01:43:59.660 --> 01:44:02.450
there is, of course,
the same facade that

01:44:02.450 --> 01:44:08.300
is burnished onto the
MIT Brass Rat, which

01:44:08.300 --> 01:44:12.470
is the name that the students
give to the ring that they get,

01:44:12.470 --> 01:44:14.720
their MIT class ring.

01:44:14.720 --> 01:44:16.910
And typically
every year there is

01:44:16.910 --> 01:44:20.120
some secret message
that is encoded

01:44:20.120 --> 01:44:23.510
in the sides of those columns.

01:44:23.510 --> 01:44:28.460
For the people who are
graduating, if their parents

01:44:28.460 --> 01:44:30.650
and friends will ask
them, they can tell them

01:44:30.650 --> 01:44:31.970
what the message is this year.

01:44:36.330 --> 01:44:42.040
SEAMANS: Okay, directly across
from MIT is Boston's Back Bay.

01:44:42.040 --> 01:44:44.260
You see the tallest
building is the Prudential

01:44:44.260 --> 01:44:47.950
Center, the Sheraton
Hotel to its right,

01:44:47.950 --> 01:44:54.010
from our vantage point, and
this is MIT's traditional view.

01:44:54.010 --> 01:44:57.700
As we mentioned earlier,
that view across the river

01:44:57.700 --> 01:45:04.702
is where MIT was
from 1864 until 1916.

01:45:04.702 --> 01:45:07.390
KEYSER: The sign
on the Sheraton--

01:45:07.390 --> 01:45:08.810
of course, if you
spell Sheraton,

01:45:08.810 --> 01:45:15.370
it's spelled S-H-E-R-A-T-O-N.
And every year during rush,

01:45:15.370 --> 01:45:19.120
it used to be that the students
would go up onto the roof

01:45:19.120 --> 01:45:22.780
of the Sheraton, the students
from ATO, an MIT fraternity,

01:45:22.780 --> 01:45:27.090
and they would block
out S-H-E-R and the N,

01:45:27.090 --> 01:45:29.740
so that the sign would
read A-T-O. Well,

01:45:29.740 --> 01:45:34.330
after years of futilely trying
to prevent the students from

01:45:34.330 --> 01:45:36.100
finding their way
up to the roof,

01:45:36.100 --> 01:45:39.760
the management of the Sheraton
agreed for safety's sake that

01:45:39.760 --> 01:45:42.770
it would be best to
block it out themselves.

01:45:42.770 --> 01:45:45.520
So during rush week, in
many years in the past,

01:45:45.520 --> 01:45:48.885
you would see just A-T-O on
the roof of the Sheraton Hotel.

01:45:48.885 --> 01:45:50.260
SEAMANS: Brightly
lit, of course,

01:45:50.260 --> 01:45:54.910
because it was the time of year
that the lights would be on.

01:45:54.910 --> 01:45:58.990
The other new building,
which changes this landscape

01:45:58.990 --> 01:46:01.570
to the left of the Prudential
as we're looking at it

01:46:01.570 --> 01:46:04.930
is one of the more phenomenal
buildings ever built in Boston.

01:46:04.930 --> 01:46:08.740
It houses primarily law firms.

01:46:08.740 --> 01:46:11.110
A lot of them moved from
the downtown section

01:46:11.110 --> 01:46:14.710
to the Back Bay to be in that
particularly new building.

01:46:18.189 --> 01:46:20.580
KEYSER: I really like
that building, Warren.

01:46:20.580 --> 01:46:22.991
I think it really does a
lot for the Boston skyline.

01:46:22.991 --> 01:46:23.490
Do you?

01:46:23.490 --> 01:46:26.250
SEAMANS: No, it's growing on me.

01:46:26.250 --> 01:46:28.590
I must admit that when
it was first going up,

01:46:28.590 --> 01:46:29.967
it wasn't that attractive.

01:46:29.967 --> 01:46:31.550
But now it's really
quite spectacular.

01:46:31.550 --> 01:46:34.380
KEYSER: Yeah, it has a kind
of an art deco feel about it.

01:46:34.380 --> 01:46:37.270
And I thought it really
was a nice punctuation.

01:46:37.270 --> 01:46:40.870
The Boston skyline, by the
way, is, in my opinion,

01:46:40.870 --> 01:46:43.620
one of the most
beautiful I've ever seen.

01:46:43.620 --> 01:46:48.420
And that includes, for example,
the spectacular skyline

01:46:48.420 --> 01:46:49.950
in Sydney, Australia.

01:46:49.950 --> 01:46:51.780
And especially at
night on a clear night,

01:46:51.780 --> 01:46:54.390
when you go across Mass Avenue
and you look to your left

01:46:54.390 --> 01:46:56.670
and you see all of
the buildings lit

01:46:56.670 --> 01:46:59.876
from the golden dome
of the City Hall

01:46:59.876 --> 01:47:01.625
all the way up past
the Prudential Center,

01:47:01.625 --> 01:47:02.850
it's just gorgeous.

01:47:08.730 --> 01:47:10.560
Now, that's a marvelous
contrast, isn't it?

01:47:10.560 --> 01:47:13.410
You see that art deco of
that new building, that dome,

01:47:13.410 --> 01:47:18.990
and here is this solid
MIT stanchion that

01:47:18.990 --> 01:47:20.066
has been here since when?

01:47:20.066 --> 01:47:20.565
1916?

01:47:20.565 --> 01:47:21.300
SEAMANS: Yes.

01:47:21.300 --> 01:47:25.010
It was dedicated in 1916,
the work of William Welles

01:47:25.010 --> 01:47:31.350
Bosworth, Class of
1889, who also was

01:47:31.350 --> 01:47:34.075
known for his work in Paris.

01:47:37.050 --> 01:47:39.780
The stage immediately
below it is

01:47:39.780 --> 01:47:43.170
where all of the dignitaries,
faculty, and deans

01:47:43.170 --> 01:47:47.070
will sit, as well as the
speakers and so forth.

01:47:47.070 --> 01:47:50.430
The kite-like thing
over the top is really

01:47:50.430 --> 01:47:53.310
a sail that was actually
designed and constructed

01:47:53.310 --> 01:47:56.730
by MIT's own Ocean
Engineering department.

01:47:56.730 --> 01:48:00.990
It has served to protect
the people sitting under it

01:48:00.990 --> 01:48:04.740
from all sorts of things--
from broiling sun to rain such

01:48:04.740 --> 01:48:06.390
as we had last year.

01:48:06.390 --> 01:48:12.150
The flowers are done by MIT's
own grounds crew each year.

01:48:12.150 --> 01:48:14.790
And they are sold
following the event

01:48:14.790 --> 01:48:19.247
to raise funds for, I believe,
MIT's community service fund.

01:48:19.247 --> 01:48:21.330
KEYSER: If you take a look
in the upper right hand

01:48:21.330 --> 01:48:25.171
corner of the screen now, you
can see the piles of diplomas.

01:48:25.171 --> 01:48:26.420
Those are the actual diplomas.

01:48:26.420 --> 01:48:31.415
SEAMANS: Yes, those red stacks
of things are MIT's diplomas.

01:48:31.415 --> 01:48:40.384
The MIT diploma has been
exactly the same since 1936.

01:48:40.384 --> 01:48:43.430
KEYSER: That is the
State Police Honor Guard

01:48:43.430 --> 01:48:45.740
that precedes every procession.

01:48:45.740 --> 01:48:49.700
It is the signal that
the academic procession

01:48:49.700 --> 01:48:51.076
is about to begin.

01:48:51.076 --> 01:48:55.170
And you can see the state police
moving forward with the colors.

01:48:55.170 --> 01:48:59.025
And they'll take a position
right in front of the stage

01:48:59.025 --> 01:48:59.566
very shortly.

01:48:59.566 --> 01:49:02.950
SEAMANS: The three flags
are, of course, the US flag,

01:49:02.950 --> 01:49:06.230
Massachusetts, and MIT's flag.

01:49:06.230 --> 01:49:11.676
KEYSER: And the MIT flag is the
one on the-- which one is it?

01:49:11.676 --> 01:49:14.598
SEAMANS: It says-- it
has Massachusetts on it.

01:49:14.598 --> 01:49:18.500
And it has also a
variation of the seal.

01:49:18.500 --> 01:49:21.036
The seal-- which we'll
see as we turn around,

01:49:21.036 --> 01:49:28.780
facing the stand where the
speaker will be speaking from--

01:49:28.780 --> 01:49:33.820
is the official version of
the official MIT seal, which

01:49:33.820 --> 01:49:38.300
was designed by MIT's founder,
William Barton Rogers in 1863,

01:49:38.300 --> 01:49:41.090
prior to the
beginning of classes.

01:49:41.090 --> 01:49:42.695
There's the view of the seal.

01:49:50.455 --> 01:49:52.920
The chairs you see
in the center there

01:49:52.920 --> 01:49:54.956
will designed by
Eduardo Catalano

01:49:54.956 --> 01:49:57.199
to be part of the
new student center,

01:49:57.199 --> 01:50:00.870
and that was the Julius
Stratton Student Center in 1966.

01:50:00.870 --> 01:50:03.350
These are the few
remaining examples

01:50:03.350 --> 01:50:06.745
of those original
chairs, by the way.

01:50:06.745 --> 01:50:09.492
KEYSER: You know,
Bosworth-- you mentioned

01:50:09.492 --> 01:50:16.780
that Bosworth had designed the
hall and the dome, of course.

01:50:16.780 --> 01:50:19.720
These policemen that you see
coming down off the stage

01:50:19.720 --> 01:50:21.504
are, in fact, not
the state police.

01:50:21.504 --> 01:50:25.250
These are actually
MIT campus policemen.

01:50:25.250 --> 01:50:26.902
That was my error.

01:50:26.902 --> 01:50:35.730
And these men work during the
day keeping the campus safe

01:50:35.730 --> 01:50:41.032
and also interacting with
the student body in as

01:50:41.032 --> 01:50:44.060
helpful a way as possible.

01:50:44.060 --> 01:50:46.240
There's a nice shot
of the diplomas now.

01:50:46.240 --> 01:50:51.610
ANNOUNCER: Ladies and gentlemen,
the academic procession,

01:50:51.610 --> 01:50:56.760
led by the Chief Marshal,
will now enter Killian Court.

01:51:02.260 --> 01:51:06.260
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

01:52:54.760 --> 01:52:56.610
SEAMANS: The procession
into the Great Court

01:52:56.610 --> 01:53:00.466
is being led by by Jim Lash, who
is President of the MIT Alumni

01:53:00.466 --> 01:53:00.966
Association.

01:53:00.966 --> 01:53:04.438
He is carrying the mace,
which is the official symbol

01:53:04.438 --> 01:53:06.422
of graduation.

01:53:06.422 --> 01:53:10.390
And we'll say a bit
more about it later.

01:53:10.390 --> 01:53:12.870
You'll notice that the--

01:53:12.870 --> 01:53:15.615
later came soon-- that
the very top of it

01:53:15.615 --> 01:53:19.072
is the beaver, which, of
course, is the mascot of MIT.

01:53:19.072 --> 01:53:21.280
And at the very bottom,
which you'll see in a minute,

01:53:21.280 --> 01:53:23.110
is an acorn.

01:53:27.583 --> 01:53:34.720
KEYSER: What you see
behind the President

01:53:34.720 --> 01:53:42.172
of the Alumni Association are
the faculty in their regalia.

01:53:42.172 --> 01:53:44.110
The regalia
represents, of course,

01:53:44.110 --> 01:53:50.110
the universities from which
they received their own degrees.

01:53:50.110 --> 01:54:02.640
And there is President Vest
is coming up the walk on the--

01:54:02.640 --> 01:54:03.935
SEAMANS: On the left hand side.

01:54:03.935 --> 01:54:06.110
KEYSER: And the gown
that he's wearing

01:54:06.110 --> 01:54:11.272
is the newly designed MIT gown,
with the crimson and gray.

01:54:18.730 --> 01:54:20.924
President Vest is
going to take his seat.

01:54:20.924 --> 01:54:23.394
And there's the
members of the faculty.

01:54:23.394 --> 01:54:26.358
That's Alec Marantz, head
of Linguistics, David Vogan,

01:54:26.358 --> 01:54:27.346
Mathematics.

01:54:34.262 --> 01:54:36.732
SEAMANS: Yeah, here we're
getting a close-up of the mace,

01:54:36.732 --> 01:54:39.696
with the mascot, the
beaver, on top of it.

01:54:39.696 --> 01:54:42.660
This was designed
by an MIT alumnus

01:54:42.660 --> 01:54:45.150
and has been the official
symbol of graduation

01:54:45.150 --> 01:54:47.640
for approximately 30 years.

01:54:56.106 --> 01:54:59.784
The faculty are represented by--

01:54:59.784 --> 01:55:03.030
50 or so of the faculty
out of a faculty of 1,000

01:55:03.030 --> 01:55:06.280
that actually sit on
the stage representing

01:55:06.280 --> 01:55:07.570
the rest of their colleagues.

01:55:07.570 --> 01:55:08.860
KEYSER: Right.

01:55:08.860 --> 01:55:10.170
I think there's actually some--

01:55:10.170 --> 01:55:11.770
the faculty is--
yeah, that's right,

01:55:11.770 --> 01:55:13.550
about a little bit under 1,000.

01:55:13.550 --> 01:55:17.050
And they're up there to
represent the entire community

01:55:17.050 --> 01:55:24.610
and to also honor
their graduates.

01:55:24.610 --> 01:55:29.050
That's Paul Lagase, the
man in the gray suit,

01:55:29.050 --> 01:55:30.210
that you're looking at now.

01:55:30.210 --> 01:55:36.580
And Paul is the Chief
Marshal of the ceremonies.

01:55:36.580 --> 01:55:42.173
And the faculty now streams
up, takes their seat.

01:55:42.173 --> 01:55:45.071
SEAMANS: Paul is also head
of the faculty committee

01:55:45.071 --> 01:55:48.690
that organizes the
commencement services.

01:56:06.402 --> 01:56:11.510
Here we see two Chairmen of
the Corporation Paul Gray

01:56:11.510 --> 01:56:14.450
is right in the center,
former president of MIT,

01:56:14.450 --> 01:56:16.550
and former chairman
of the Corporation.

01:56:16.550 --> 01:56:17.689
And to his right--

01:56:17.689 --> 01:56:19.730
KEYSER: Alex d'Arbeloff,
who is the current Chair

01:56:19.730 --> 01:56:21.380
of the Corporation.

01:56:21.380 --> 01:56:23.416
SEAMANS: Stepping
down right now--

01:56:23.416 --> 01:56:24.290
KEYSER: That's right.

01:56:24.290 --> 01:56:28.170
SEAMANS: --to turn over the
reins to another chairman.

01:56:39.340 --> 01:56:42.220
The City of Cambridge has always
represented these services.

01:56:42.220 --> 01:56:47.060
That's Mayor Sullivan from
the good city of Cambridge.

01:56:47.060 --> 01:56:49.010
KEYSER: That's Michael Sullivan.

01:56:49.010 --> 01:56:54.990
And I believe his father
was Walter J. Sullivan, who

01:56:54.990 --> 01:56:56.630
lives in Cambridge now.

01:56:56.630 --> 01:56:58.160
He lives on Prospect Street.

01:57:03.220 --> 01:57:06.730
SEAMANS: Each year the 50
year class, the class that

01:57:06.730 --> 01:57:12.520
graduated 50 years ago, is
represented in the commencement

01:57:12.520 --> 01:57:16.710
parade by all the members
attending their 50th reunion.

01:57:16.710 --> 01:57:20.400
You will also notice they're
carrying the '53 flag.

01:57:20.400 --> 01:57:23.190
And to the right of the stage
when you're looking at it,

01:57:23.190 --> 01:57:25.770
there is the class
of '03's flag.

01:57:25.770 --> 01:57:29.030
Each class has at least
two different flags.

01:57:29.030 --> 01:57:31.770
You saw a short one being
carried by this group coming

01:57:31.770 --> 01:57:34.795
in, and the one on
the left of the stage

01:57:34.795 --> 01:57:37.680
is the traditional one that
joins all the other class

01:57:37.680 --> 01:57:39.160
flags.

01:57:39.160 --> 01:57:42.960
The red coat that
you see is the honor

01:57:42.960 --> 01:57:47.140
that is bestowed upon
members of the 50-year class.

01:57:47.140 --> 01:57:49.770
It is cardinal red.

01:57:49.770 --> 01:57:54.020
And some of the ties you
see are the old freshmen tie

01:57:54.020 --> 01:57:59.420
which represents the colors of
cardinal red and silver gray.

01:57:59.420 --> 01:58:01.100
KEYSER: Although you
can't see them now,

01:58:01.100 --> 01:58:03.530
but you may have caught
a glimpse of him earlier,

01:58:03.530 --> 01:58:06.610
Warren is wearing exactly
that outfit right now.

01:58:06.610 --> 01:58:13.765
He has a red jacket and also
a striped red and gray tie.

01:58:13.765 --> 01:58:16.450
SEAMANS: I should explain
that I am an honorary member

01:58:16.450 --> 01:58:18.810
of the class of
1935, which is why I

01:58:18.810 --> 01:58:21.102
am allowed to wear a red coat.

01:58:21.102 --> 01:58:22.060
KEYSER: My god, Warren.

01:58:22.060 --> 01:58:24.380
I was born in 1935.

01:58:24.380 --> 01:58:24.880
So were you?

01:58:24.880 --> 01:58:25.760
SEAMANS: Me too.

01:58:25.760 --> 01:58:26.500
KEYSER: Oh, I'll be darned.

01:58:26.500 --> 01:58:27.375
What's your birthday?

01:58:27.375 --> 01:58:28.532
SEAMANS: August 8.

01:58:28.532 --> 01:58:29.240
KEYSER: August 8?

01:58:29.240 --> 01:58:31.166
I'm July 7.

01:58:31.166 --> 01:58:35.950
Now, there's a guy
wearing glasses 2003.

01:58:35.950 --> 01:58:38.310
I wish I could identify him,
but I don't recognize him.

01:58:38.310 --> 01:58:39.737
But those are
really cool glasses.

01:58:39.737 --> 01:58:40.945
SEAMANS: Those certainly are.

01:58:46.390 --> 01:58:49.360
There's a record turnout
for the 50-year class.

01:58:49.360 --> 01:58:53.390
Starting in this period,
this is the period

01:58:53.390 --> 01:58:55.520
immediately following
most of the returning

01:58:55.520 --> 01:58:57.925
veterans from World
War II had graduated.

01:58:57.925 --> 01:59:02.800
So these now tend to be
younger than the classes--

01:59:02.800 --> 01:59:04.925
the average age of
these 50-year classes

01:59:04.925 --> 01:59:08.720
is younger than it has been
for the last several years.

01:59:08.720 --> 01:59:12.040
KEYSER: We should probably
mention that this telecast is

01:59:12.040 --> 01:59:14.978
going out not only to
the 30 monitors located

01:59:14.978 --> 01:59:20.150
all around the MIT campus
for those people who are not

01:59:20.150 --> 01:59:22.490
sitting in the Court
itself, but it's actually

01:59:22.490 --> 01:59:27.490
going all around the world,
from Kyoto to Carlsbad.

01:59:27.490 --> 01:59:31.697
Warren and I probably have
an audience of hundreds--

01:59:31.697 --> 01:59:33.010
SEAMANS: At least.

01:59:33.010 --> 01:59:36.400
KEYSER: --out there listening
to us comment on this.

01:59:36.400 --> 01:59:38.460
But to all of you out
there, wherever you are,

01:59:38.460 --> 01:59:39.420
we say hello to you.

01:59:39.420 --> 01:59:43.000
And welcome to the
MIT world webcast.

01:59:43.000 --> 01:59:48.170
ANNOUNCER: Ladies and
gentlemen, the guests of honor,

01:59:48.170 --> 01:59:51.710
the Class of 2003.

01:59:51.710 --> 01:59:53.990
[APPLAUSE]

01:59:55.358 --> 01:59:59.068
KEYSER: There's a lovely
shot over the shoulders

01:59:59.068 --> 02:00:01.056
of the faculty.

02:00:01.056 --> 02:00:04.535
And the students will be
coming up the center aisle.

02:00:04.535 --> 02:00:07.517
SEAMANS: The 50-year class
have an honored place this year

02:00:07.517 --> 02:00:11.990
to the left of the stage,
looking away from the stage.

02:00:15.469 --> 02:00:17.954
KEYSER: There is the
procession beginning.

02:00:17.954 --> 02:00:21.930
And the two men at the
head of the procession

02:00:21.930 --> 02:00:25.409
are the deans of Student
Affairs on the right-- that's

02:00:25.409 --> 02:00:26.900
Larry Benedict.

02:00:26.900 --> 02:00:30.627
And on the left is
Bob Redwine, who's

02:00:30.627 --> 02:00:33.361
Dean of Undergraduate Education.

02:00:33.361 --> 02:00:36.880
This is the second or
third year now, I think,

02:00:36.880 --> 02:00:42.770
that MIT has held these
two dual functions,

02:00:42.770 --> 02:00:45.590
taking the undergraduate office
and dividing it up in this way.

02:00:45.590 --> 02:00:48.376
And it's been a
remarkable success.

02:00:48.376 --> 02:00:50.352
Both Dean Redwine
and Dean Benedict

02:00:50.352 --> 02:00:56.350
have just done a superb job in
meeting the needs of the MIT

02:00:56.350 --> 02:00:57.994
undergraduate student body.

02:00:57.994 --> 02:01:00.396
We're really very lucky
to have them here.

02:01:00.396 --> 02:01:04.334
And as you can see behind
them are the graduates

02:01:04.334 --> 02:01:09.590
of the Class of 2003.

02:01:09.590 --> 02:01:12.350
SEAMANS: And this year,
nearly 2,500 diplomas

02:01:12.350 --> 02:01:17.870
will be given to both the grads
and undergraduate students.

02:01:17.870 --> 02:01:22.690
And that is-- it's
very important

02:01:22.690 --> 02:01:25.600
that all of these people
come in and sit exactly

02:01:25.600 --> 02:01:30.470
in the right chair, because they
file up to get their diploma,

02:01:30.470 --> 02:01:33.305
they have to be in the right
position to get their diploma.

02:01:33.305 --> 02:01:36.260
It's another tradition
that every student who

02:01:36.260 --> 02:01:40.452
graduates from MIT, who
attends a graduation ceremony,

02:01:40.452 --> 02:01:43.700
is handed his
diploma personally.

02:01:43.700 --> 02:01:45.840
The president
literally shakes hands

02:01:45.840 --> 02:01:48.918
with every person
who gets a degree

02:01:48.918 --> 02:01:50.690
from an undergraduate course.

02:01:50.690 --> 02:01:54.620
And the provost, who is
the chief academic officer,

02:01:54.620 --> 02:01:58.580
hands the diploma handshakes
to the person graduating

02:01:58.580 --> 02:02:01.340
with a graduate degree.

02:02:01.340 --> 02:02:02.840
KEYSER: Over the
years, there's been

02:02:02.840 --> 02:02:09.440
a longstanding concern about the
size of the graduating class.

02:02:09.440 --> 02:02:12.130
And do we really want
everyone to come up and get

02:02:12.130 --> 02:02:13.010
their degree?

02:02:13.010 --> 02:02:16.040
In many schools, they
actually divide the class up

02:02:16.040 --> 02:02:19.824
into schools and have a
vulcanized graduation.

02:02:19.824 --> 02:02:23.296
But MIT thinks that it's
important for everyone

02:02:23.296 --> 02:02:24.784
to be recognized.

02:02:24.784 --> 02:02:28.752
And we've done--
about 10 years ago,

02:02:28.752 --> 02:02:32.720
we introduced this dual system,
where the students will be

02:02:32.720 --> 02:02:36.522
coming up-- two, perhaps, one
on the left and one on the right

02:02:36.522 --> 02:02:38.043
side of the stage--

02:02:38.043 --> 02:02:39.168
and they'll be interweaved.

02:02:39.168 --> 02:02:42.640
And it actually is
extremely efficient.

02:02:42.640 --> 02:02:46.382
And everybody gets their day
in the sun-- or at least today,

02:02:46.382 --> 02:02:48.407
their day in the shade.

02:02:48.407 --> 02:02:49.990
SEAMANS: And every
person, in addition

02:02:49.990 --> 02:02:51.781
to getting their diploma,
has their picture

02:02:51.781 --> 02:02:55.480
taken receiving a diploma.

02:02:55.480 --> 02:03:00.212
So it's all very much
part of the system.

02:03:00.212 --> 02:03:03.176
It works very well for MIT.

02:03:03.176 --> 02:03:06.140
There's Larry Benedict on the
right, Bob Redwine on the left

02:03:06.140 --> 02:03:07.807
now, approaching the ramps.

02:03:07.807 --> 02:03:09.598
They'll probably split
off, left and right.

02:03:19.231 --> 02:03:23.330
MIT is very proud of the
fact that approximately 40%

02:03:23.330 --> 02:03:26.613
of the graduating
class is women.

02:03:26.613 --> 02:03:30.985
And this has crept
up over the years

02:03:30.985 --> 02:03:34.137
to where it is something
that MIT is not only

02:03:34.137 --> 02:03:38.668
very happy about, but it is a
wonderful sign of the times.

02:03:38.668 --> 02:03:41.632
KEYSER: Yes, that's
absolutely right.

02:03:41.632 --> 02:03:45.090
And the percentage
of women this year

02:03:45.090 --> 02:03:49.042
has gone up incredibly
during my years at MIT.

02:03:52.006 --> 02:03:56.699
And it's been a wonderful move,
not only because of the justice

02:03:56.699 --> 02:04:02.874
of it, but it turns out that the
cost of vandalism in the dorms

02:04:02.874 --> 02:04:04.490
went down.

02:04:04.490 --> 02:04:06.780
Women tend to [INAUDIBLE].

02:04:06.780 --> 02:04:10.320
SEAMANS: Very much of a
civilizing and healthy effect.

02:04:10.320 --> 02:04:12.100
I think it's also
important to recognize

02:04:12.100 --> 02:04:15.350
that students from
nearly 100 countries

02:04:15.350 --> 02:04:17.240
are represented in
the student body.

02:04:17.240 --> 02:04:19.460
And, of course, those
will be represented

02:04:19.460 --> 02:04:24.750
here today, those same country.

02:04:24.750 --> 02:04:27.822
KEYSER: I know everybody
likes a sunny day

02:04:27.822 --> 02:04:29.806
because it is so pretty.

02:04:29.806 --> 02:04:34.022
But I must say that
having sat through-- oh,

02:04:34.022 --> 02:04:38.740
maybe 20 commencements myself--

02:04:38.740 --> 02:04:41.288
when there are sunny days,
they can be really brutal

02:04:41.288 --> 02:04:44.940
on the students, because
the sun is merciless.

02:04:44.940 --> 02:04:47.046
And a day like today,
it seems to me,

02:04:47.046 --> 02:04:51.650
is really very, very
desirable because it's dry,

02:04:51.650 --> 02:04:52.730
but it's pleasant.

02:04:52.730 --> 02:04:56.600
SEAMANS: For those of you who
are not in the Cambridge area,

02:04:56.600 --> 02:05:01.240
it has been on the verge of
seeming to rain almost all day.

02:05:01.240 --> 02:05:03.240
And we've had a very
miserable spring here.

02:05:03.240 --> 02:05:06.440
But yesterday it
was sort of nice.

02:05:06.440 --> 02:05:07.870
Today is sort of nice.

02:05:07.870 --> 02:05:09.951
Saturday was a disaster.

02:05:09.951 --> 02:05:11.669
Had this been on
Saturday, we would have

02:05:11.669 --> 02:05:13.388
been underwater the entire day.

02:05:16.825 --> 02:05:20.753
KEYSER: The people that you see
now, the ones with the hoods,

02:05:20.753 --> 02:05:24.540
are people who have
received their hoods

02:05:24.540 --> 02:05:27.846
at a special hooding ceremony
that MIT does for all

02:05:27.846 --> 02:05:30.442
of its doctoral students.

02:05:30.442 --> 02:05:34.414
That actually occurred yesterday
in the Howard Johnson Athletic

02:05:34.414 --> 02:05:36.070
Center.

02:05:36.070 --> 02:05:38.336
And it's really a
very nice ceremony.

02:05:38.336 --> 02:05:39.824
It's an informal ceremony.

02:05:39.824 --> 02:05:42.304
The families of all of
the doctoral students

02:05:42.304 --> 02:05:48.610
come to watch their offspring
receive their PhD hoods.

02:05:48.610 --> 02:05:52.264
But it also is, once
again, a time-saving thing.

02:05:52.264 --> 02:05:54.659
Because we no
longer need to worry

02:05:54.659 --> 02:05:58.770
about having to hood
them on the stage itself.

02:05:58.770 --> 02:06:01.080
So this is a
typical way in which

02:06:01.080 --> 02:06:05.320
MIT turns lemons into lemonade.

02:06:05.320 --> 02:06:07.296
It's really a
wonderful occasion.

02:06:12.934 --> 02:06:15.184
SEAMANS: Okay, here we have
some shots of the faculty.

02:06:15.184 --> 02:06:17.156
KEYSER: That's
Stanford Anderson.

02:06:17.156 --> 02:06:19.621
And the man you see
now with the black hat

02:06:19.621 --> 02:06:23.072
with the mustache,
that's Rafael Bras.

02:06:23.072 --> 02:06:26.030
And he is going to be the
new Chair of the Faculty,

02:06:26.030 --> 02:06:29.974
replacing Steve Graves
who just stepped down.

02:06:29.974 --> 02:06:35.397
There's Jay Fay in
Engineering next to him.

02:06:35.397 --> 02:06:38.108
And let's see.

02:06:38.108 --> 02:06:39.341
Who else do I recognize?

02:06:39.341 --> 02:06:41.895
As I get older and they get--
there's John de Monchaux.

02:06:41.895 --> 02:06:44.020
SEAMANS: Former Dean of
the School of Architecture.

02:06:44.020 --> 02:06:45.002
KEYSER: Right.

02:06:45.002 --> 02:06:45.984
And who's that?

02:06:48.930 --> 02:06:51.385
Who else do I know?

02:06:51.385 --> 02:06:53.840
Oh, there's Gabriel
Bitran, that's right.

02:06:53.840 --> 02:06:56.295
Right, Subra Suresh.

02:06:56.295 --> 02:06:58.270
Gabriel Bitran is Sloan school.

02:06:58.270 --> 02:07:04.226
Subra Suresh is-- there's
Dan Hastings in the middle

02:07:04.226 --> 02:07:05.723
there, with the glasses.

02:07:05.723 --> 02:07:08.717
And he is also Engineering.

02:07:08.717 --> 02:07:10.214
There's Eleanor Westney.

02:07:10.214 --> 02:07:12.210
She is a marshal, and
she's looking so proud

02:07:12.210 --> 02:07:13.208
and chipper there.

02:07:13.208 --> 02:07:16.701
Eleanor is an expert
on Japanese industry.

02:07:16.701 --> 02:07:18.198
She's in the Sloan school.

02:07:22.689 --> 02:07:24.685
SEAMANS: Okay, we're
back at the mace,

02:07:24.685 --> 02:07:30.673
with this view representing
the tradition of commencement,

02:07:30.673 --> 02:07:35.663
which they signify
[INAUDIBLE] in all its glory.

02:07:47.170 --> 02:07:54.442
The Corporation is the
governing body of MIT

02:07:54.442 --> 02:07:59.295
of approximately 100 people
from all walks of life.

02:07:59.295 --> 02:08:01.770
Only some of them are alumni.

02:08:01.770 --> 02:08:06.472
And these are-- we're now
getting a view of some

02:08:06.472 --> 02:08:08.205
of the Corporation.

02:08:11.175 --> 02:08:15.180
[PROCESSIONAL MUSIC]

02:09:37.154 --> 02:09:40.640
KEYSER: Well, our
speaker this morning--

02:09:40.640 --> 02:09:44.375
it's always a question
of great suspense at MIT,

02:09:44.375 --> 02:09:46.118
who the speaker is going to be.

02:09:46.118 --> 02:09:49.590
And the president,
there's a shot

02:09:49.590 --> 02:09:52.830
of Chuck Vest now on the
left, looking at the program.

02:09:52.830 --> 02:09:58.445
Chuck gets input from all
kinds of sources at MIT.

02:09:58.445 --> 02:10:04.925
And it's always a surprise and
often an occasion of delight

02:10:04.925 --> 02:10:08.532
to see who got it right.

02:10:08.532 --> 02:10:11.710
And our speaker
this time is someone

02:10:11.710 --> 02:10:13.840
who needs no introduction.

02:10:13.840 --> 02:10:17.170
Everybody knows George Mitchell.

02:10:17.170 --> 02:10:20.335
SEAMANS: Former
Senator from Maine,

02:10:20.335 --> 02:10:27.466
who also is the peace leader
for the North Ireland settlement

02:10:27.466 --> 02:10:29.458
that is still sort of in effect.

02:10:29.458 --> 02:10:32.446
He was a longtime
Senator from Maine,

02:10:32.446 --> 02:10:35.784
and one of the greats of the--

02:10:35.784 --> 02:10:38.110
I wish we had more like
him in the Senate today.

02:10:38.110 --> 02:10:41.214
KEYSER: Yes, absolutely.

02:10:41.214 --> 02:10:48.190
Two years ago, our speakers were
Click and Clack, the Car Guys.

02:10:48.190 --> 02:10:53.635
So this is from the
sublime to the ridiculous.

02:10:53.635 --> 02:10:55.890
SEAMANS: Well,
[INAUDIBLE] ridiculous.

02:10:55.890 --> 02:10:58.700
KEYSER: From the
ridiculous to the sublime.

02:10:58.700 --> 02:11:03.755
SEAMANS: I think we've had
a couple nice shots of Chuck

02:11:03.755 --> 02:11:04.255
Vest.

02:11:06.800 --> 02:11:08.800
Chuck has been
president now for--

02:11:08.800 --> 02:11:11.394
I think this is his 11th year.

02:11:11.394 --> 02:11:13.774
The Institute has gone
through phenomenal change

02:11:13.774 --> 02:11:17.106
during that period of time.

02:11:17.106 --> 02:11:20.960
We announced earlier that
there is more than $1 million

02:11:20.960 --> 02:11:25.220
worth of construction taking
place, or nearing completion

02:11:25.220 --> 02:11:26.780
on the MIT campus.

02:11:26.780 --> 02:11:29.244
Nearly 1 million square
feet have been added,

02:11:29.244 --> 02:11:34.711
primarily to make living easier
for the community, particularly

02:11:34.711 --> 02:11:37.196
student housing and
student facility.

02:11:37.196 --> 02:11:39.681
There's a brand-new
fitness center,

02:11:39.681 --> 02:11:42.663
which is a tremendous
addition to the campus.

02:11:45.645 --> 02:11:48.560
KEYSER: As I see the
students walking by,

02:11:48.560 --> 02:11:57.200
you'll notice how conservative
the student body is dressed.

02:11:57.200 --> 02:12:00.010
Every so often, you'll
see a piece of color.

02:12:00.010 --> 02:12:04.850
There's a young man, the
Class of 2003, with a scarf.

02:12:04.850 --> 02:12:10.230
But decorating their costume,
the academic costume,

02:12:10.230 --> 02:12:12.330
is not so much in fashion.

02:12:12.330 --> 02:12:14.490
SEAMANS: But also the
haircuts and so forth are

02:12:14.490 --> 02:12:17.100
far more conservative
than they were

02:12:17.100 --> 02:12:20.052
three years ago or 10 years
ago or certainly 20 years ago.

02:12:20.052 --> 02:12:21.525
KEYSER: Absolutely.

02:12:21.525 --> 02:12:23.489
Now, let's see--

02:12:23.489 --> 02:12:25.698
SEAMANS: That my be a
sign of the economic times

02:12:25.698 --> 02:12:29.872
as well, because
who knows what--

02:12:29.872 --> 02:12:32.490
I'm sure everybody
of this group who

02:12:32.490 --> 02:12:34.782
wants a job in the
outside world will

02:12:34.782 --> 02:12:37.237
have gotten one by this
time, or be admitted

02:12:37.237 --> 02:12:38.652
to their additional schools.

02:12:38.652 --> 02:12:40.360
KEYSER: You wonder
what's in their hearts

02:12:40.360 --> 02:12:44.240
as they walk up
here, because they're

02:12:44.240 --> 02:12:47.746
leaving four years
of a university which

02:12:47.746 --> 02:12:51.971
has, of course, got to
be one of the hardest

02:12:51.971 --> 02:12:55.065
academic grinds in the world.

02:12:55.065 --> 02:12:59.166
And yet it's what they do,
and what they do very well.

02:12:59.166 --> 02:13:00.162
SEAMANS: Our treasurer.

02:13:00.162 --> 02:13:02.652
KEYSER: There's our
treasurer, Allan Bufferd,

02:13:02.652 --> 02:13:04.644
disappearing out of the right.

02:13:08.587 --> 02:13:11.170
So the students, it's got to be
a bittersweet moment for them,

02:13:11.170 --> 02:13:14.150
because they're
leaving a marvelous

02:13:14.150 --> 02:13:16.550
environment, some place
where they can [INAUDIBLE]..

02:13:16.550 --> 02:13:18.716
SEAMANS: There's a Corporation
member, [INAUDIBLE]..

02:13:18.716 --> 02:13:19.720
KEYSER: Oh, yes.

02:13:19.720 --> 02:13:20.720
Those are three members.

02:13:20.720 --> 02:13:21.758
SEAMANS: Class of '45.

02:13:25.690 --> 02:13:27.940
KEYSER: And it's a bittersweet
moment for the student.

02:13:27.940 --> 02:13:28.240
SEAMANS: [INAUDIBLE].

02:13:28.240 --> 02:13:30.610
KEYSER: Oh, there's in the
back, putting his hat on,

02:13:30.610 --> 02:13:31.916
that's Steve Lerman.

02:13:31.916 --> 02:13:32.624
SEAMANS: Oh, yes.

02:13:32.624 --> 02:13:37.000
KEYSER: He's a house master
at the new graduate dorm

02:13:37.000 --> 02:13:38.290
on, I think, Albany Street.

02:13:38.290 --> 02:13:40.370
I think they call
it the warehouse.

02:13:40.370 --> 02:13:42.380
That's Ros Williams,
her hand to her chin.

02:13:42.380 --> 02:13:45.240
She used to be Dean of
Undergraduate Education.

02:13:45.240 --> 02:13:47.630
She's Science
Technology Society.

02:13:47.630 --> 02:13:51.869
That's Wesley Harris
behind with the orange--

02:13:51.869 --> 02:13:52.410
SEAMANS: Vest

02:13:52.410 --> 02:13:53.844
KEYSER: --hood.

02:13:53.844 --> 02:13:56.572
And he's talking
to Steve Lippard,

02:13:56.572 --> 02:14:00.340
who is the head of the
Department of Chemistry.

02:14:03.640 --> 02:14:05.900
I was talking to
Steve the other day.

02:14:05.900 --> 02:14:10.150
I mean, he's one of the
country's great chemists.

02:14:10.150 --> 02:14:12.960
And he told me that he had
majored in English literature

02:14:12.960 --> 02:14:15.380
in undergraduate,
as an undergraduate.

02:14:15.380 --> 02:14:18.720
He always wondered--
there's somebody with a--

02:14:18.720 --> 02:14:21.527
there, he's got a little
teddy bear on his [INAUDIBLE]..

02:14:21.527 --> 02:14:22.501
Good for you.

02:14:22.501 --> 02:14:25.910
There's somebody holding a--

02:14:25.910 --> 02:14:27.371
no, just a tassel.

02:14:31.280 --> 02:14:33.930
SEAMANS: Okay, the
stage mysteriously

02:14:33.930 --> 02:14:39.390
appears here in Killian Court
every year, about 10 days ahead

02:14:39.390 --> 02:14:41.430
of the commencement services.

02:14:41.430 --> 02:14:44.070
And then a day later,
it totally disappears.

02:14:44.070 --> 02:14:48.300
It is a phenomenal
piece of a jigsaw puzzle

02:14:48.300 --> 02:14:51.165
that goes together so
readily and so easily

02:14:51.165 --> 02:14:54.750
that it seems almost like
a miracle that appears,

02:14:54.750 --> 02:14:57.305
and then suddenly it's gone.

02:14:57.305 --> 02:14:58.680
KEYSER: Warren
and I are actually

02:14:58.680 --> 02:15:01.500
sitting underneath
that stage right now.

02:15:01.500 --> 02:15:04.080
The faculty is
right on top of us.

02:15:04.080 --> 02:15:07.590
And sitting here, we
have a beautiful view

02:15:07.590 --> 02:15:12.210
of the joists that
hold up the stage.

02:15:12.210 --> 02:15:15.400
And I can't help but wishing
that my house were as well

02:15:15.400 --> 02:15:16.725
built as this stage.

02:15:16.725 --> 02:15:21.570
SEAMANS: You see the Class of
2003's flag just to the right,

02:15:21.570 --> 02:15:23.010
as it's moving off screen.

02:15:28.926 --> 02:15:33.363
KEYSER: There's one of MIT's
favorite venues for hacks.

02:15:33.363 --> 02:15:36.321
Of course, the great
hack was the police car

02:15:36.321 --> 02:15:38.786
on the dome about
five or six years ago,

02:15:38.786 --> 02:15:40.265
when the MIT community--

02:15:40.265 --> 02:15:42.432
SEAMANS: I hate to tell
you, Jay, it's 11 years ago.

02:15:42.432 --> 02:15:43.056
KEYSER: Oh, no.

02:15:43.056 --> 02:15:43.940
Was it 11 years?

02:15:43.940 --> 02:15:44.690
SEAMANS: 11 years.

02:15:44.690 --> 02:15:45.620
KEYSER: Oh, god.

02:15:45.620 --> 02:15:49.115
SEAMANS: But one thing we
need to comment perhaps on

02:15:49.115 --> 02:15:52.835
is, those who are
graduating with a degree

02:15:52.835 --> 02:15:58.507
from a commissioning from one
of the US military services

02:15:58.507 --> 02:15:59.340
wear those uniforms.

02:15:59.340 --> 02:16:01.220
You see the Navy in white.

02:16:01.220 --> 02:16:06.140
You saw somebody with
an Air Force cap before.

02:16:06.140 --> 02:16:12.214
So that is part of the tradition
as well, not only here,

02:16:12.214 --> 02:16:13.399
but in every school.

02:16:13.399 --> 02:16:15.190
KEYSER: I think we have
all three services.

02:16:15.190 --> 02:16:15.686
SEAMANS: Yes, we do.

02:16:15.686 --> 02:16:17.174
KEYSER: Army,
Navy, and Air Force

02:16:17.174 --> 02:16:19.158
ROTC, and they're graduating.

02:16:27.094 --> 02:16:31.062
There's Michael Sullivan
next to Paul Gray.

02:16:31.062 --> 02:16:32.550
And in the lower
left hand corner,

02:16:32.550 --> 02:16:38.006
you can see the shot there of
Allan Bufferd, the Institute

02:16:38.006 --> 02:16:39.494
treasurer.

02:16:39.494 --> 02:16:41.478
And Michael Sullivan
is, of course,

02:16:41.478 --> 02:16:42.966
the one without the hat.

02:16:47.430 --> 02:16:50.406
You could see Paul Gray
leaning over talking to him.

02:16:50.406 --> 02:16:53.382
He was probably trying to
get a parking ticket fixed.

02:16:59.920 --> 02:17:02.780
And here's a nice shot
over the shoulders.

02:17:02.780 --> 02:17:06.201
There's Tom Magnanti
on the left there.

02:17:06.201 --> 02:17:08.330
He's the Dean of the
School of Engineering.

02:17:08.330 --> 02:17:10.230
Warren, I'm surprised
that the MIT

02:17:10.230 --> 02:17:12.585
flag doesn't have gray in it.

02:17:12.585 --> 02:17:16.689
SEAMANS: I am surprised as well.

02:17:16.689 --> 02:17:19.752
KEYSER: In fact, it's
neither crimson nor gray.

02:17:19.752 --> 02:17:20.738
No, I agree.

02:17:20.738 --> 02:17:23.696
SEAMANS: It looks sort
of like a salmon color.

02:17:30.598 --> 02:17:33.559
KEYSER: There's Brit d'Arbeloff,
Alex d'Arbeloff's spouse.

02:17:33.559 --> 02:17:36.862
He's the Chairman of the
Corporation now stepping down.

02:17:36.862 --> 02:17:39.650
SEAMANS: And also an alumna.

02:17:39.650 --> 02:17:41.119
KEYSER: She's an
engineer, I think.

02:17:41.119 --> 02:17:42.079
SEAMANS: Yes.

02:17:42.079 --> 02:17:44.479
KEYSER: From the
School of Engineering.

02:17:47.359 --> 02:17:53.164
SEAMANS: Coming to Becky Vest,
current First Lady of MIT.

02:17:53.164 --> 02:17:58.400
This is Priscilla
Gray, who has--

02:17:58.400 --> 02:18:00.609
obviously Paul Gray's wife.

02:18:00.609 --> 02:18:05.339
And so she has been both First
Lady and First Corporation

02:18:05.339 --> 02:18:06.320
woman.

02:18:06.320 --> 02:18:09.059
KEYSER: Laura Mersky is
sitting next to Priscilla.

02:18:09.059 --> 02:18:13.505
And Laura is President
Vest's special assistant.

02:18:13.505 --> 02:18:16.963
And next to Laura
is Paul Parravano,

02:18:16.963 --> 02:18:20.930
who is MIT Liaison with
the town of Cambridge.

02:18:20.930 --> 02:18:25.129
And next to Paul is [INAUDIBLE].

02:18:25.129 --> 02:18:30.162
He works for Gail Gallagher,
who is the éminence grise

02:18:30.162 --> 02:18:31.168
of the commencement.

02:18:31.168 --> 02:18:31.668
She is the--

02:18:31.668 --> 02:18:32.156
SEAMANS: The major-domo.

02:18:32.156 --> 02:18:33.322
She tells us all what to do.

02:18:33.322 --> 02:18:36.548
KEYSER: She's the one
who makes it all happen.

02:18:36.548 --> 02:18:37.524
There's a lei.

02:18:37.524 --> 02:18:39.476
SEAMANS: Obviously we
know where she's from.

02:18:42.898 --> 02:18:44.356
There's somebody
with a cell phone.

02:18:44.356 --> 02:18:46.897
KEYSER: There's somebody with
a decorated tab, but we can't--

02:18:46.897 --> 02:18:49.250
there's another lei.

02:18:49.250 --> 02:18:54.690
SEAMANS: We do have a large
number of students from Hawaii.

02:18:54.690 --> 02:19:00.865
Hawaii has some absolutely
superb school systems,

02:19:00.865 --> 02:19:03.358
and they send a
disproportionately high number

02:19:03.358 --> 02:19:05.353
of students to MIT.

02:19:05.353 --> 02:19:06.764
KEYSER: From Hawaii?

02:19:06.764 --> 02:19:07.305
SEAMANS: Yes.

02:19:07.305 --> 02:19:08.769
KEYSER: Have you
ever visited Hawaii?

02:19:08.769 --> 02:19:09.745
SEAMANS: No, I haven't.

02:19:09.745 --> 02:19:10.703
KEYSER: Neither have I.

02:19:10.703 --> 02:19:13.656
SEAMANS: But over the
years, as freshman advisees,

02:19:13.656 --> 02:19:15.508
it seems like every
other year, I always

02:19:15.508 --> 02:19:18.208
have somebody from Hawaii.

02:19:18.208 --> 02:19:21.737
KEYSER: Some more PhDs.

02:19:21.737 --> 02:19:26.074
[INAUDIBLE] gold hood.

02:19:26.074 --> 02:19:27.571
It's Doctor of Science, I think.

02:19:34.559 --> 02:19:36.400
SEAMANS: Just again,
briefly, those

02:19:36.400 --> 02:19:38.950
who are watching
as we get through--

02:19:38.950 --> 02:19:41.889
we will be leaving you as soon
as the commencement services

02:19:41.889 --> 02:19:43.719
start.

02:19:43.719 --> 02:19:45.670
Then there will be
the introductions

02:19:45.670 --> 02:19:51.700
of various of the senior
class, of the 50-year class.

02:19:51.700 --> 02:19:56.620
There will be Chuck Vest's
charge to the students,

02:19:56.620 --> 02:20:00.290
plus the keynote speech
by George Mitchell,

02:20:00.290 --> 02:20:02.395
not necessarily in that order.

02:20:02.395 --> 02:20:07.430
Then they will start
distributing the diplomas,

02:20:07.430 --> 02:20:10.230
and they will be
done simultaneously.

02:20:10.230 --> 02:20:13.233
The Grad School and the
Undergraduate School

02:20:13.233 --> 02:20:17.500
are going up individually to
receive their diplomas directly

02:20:17.500 --> 02:20:21.445
from either their
president or the provost.

02:20:21.445 --> 02:20:23.738
It is also one more
tradition, it's

02:20:23.738 --> 02:20:25.130
interesting to
note, we've talked

02:20:25.130 --> 02:20:27.860
about the ring, the brass
rat, that a good number

02:20:27.860 --> 02:20:29.660
of the students wear.

02:20:29.660 --> 02:20:32.626
It's a tradition that
immediately following

02:20:32.626 --> 02:20:35.972
the receipt of the
diploma, the ring

02:20:35.972 --> 02:20:39.796
is turned around so that
the beaver faces the world.

02:20:39.796 --> 02:20:45.834
As an undergraduate, the beaver
faces the bearer of the ring.

02:20:45.834 --> 02:20:47.268
This is a longstanding
tradition,

02:20:47.268 --> 02:20:50.136
and it has something
to do with--

02:20:50.136 --> 02:20:52.946
KEYSER: Look at that--
four cell phones.

02:20:52.946 --> 02:20:54.196
Right there, four cell phones.

02:20:54.196 --> 02:20:55.000
They're all talking
on cell phones.

02:20:55.000 --> 02:20:56.102
Now there's a change.

02:20:56.102 --> 02:20:58.269
Look, five-- one, two,
three, four, five.

02:20:58.269 --> 02:20:59.810
Who in the world
are they talking to?

02:20:59.810 --> 02:21:02.270
I mean, look, six cell phones!

02:21:02.270 --> 02:21:04.392
My god.

02:21:04.392 --> 02:21:06.100
Look, there'll be even
seven cell phones.

02:21:06.100 --> 02:21:07.740
They'll even be
talking cell phones

02:21:07.740 --> 02:21:09.445
as they walk up on the stage.

02:21:09.445 --> 02:21:12.174
SEAMANS: Can't miss-- look how--

02:21:12.174 --> 02:21:12.840
KEYSER: Amazing.

02:21:12.840 --> 02:21:14.923
SEAMANS: I assume they're
talking to their parents

02:21:14.923 --> 02:21:16.345
in the back rows and so forth.

02:21:16.345 --> 02:21:17.220
KEYSER: Amazing shot.

02:21:17.220 --> 02:21:18.140
SEAMANS: Who knows?

02:21:18.140 --> 02:21:19.572
That is a change that has--

02:21:19.572 --> 02:21:20.780
KEYSER: That's a real change.

02:21:20.780 --> 02:21:23.280
SEAMANS: --you didn't notice
last year or previous years.

02:21:23.280 --> 02:21:23.865
KEYSER: Right.

02:21:23.865 --> 02:21:27.742
Those are the Navy
graduates, Navy ROTC.

02:21:30.694 --> 02:21:31.678
SEAMANS: Another phone.

02:21:31.678 --> 02:21:33.154
KEYSER: Another cell phone.

02:21:36.767 --> 02:21:39.058
SEAMANS: I wonder if we'll
have to make an announcement

02:21:39.058 --> 02:21:40.534
to have everybody turn
their cell phone--

02:21:40.534 --> 02:21:41.272
KEYSER: I'll be
you they're going

02:21:41.272 --> 02:21:42.256
to have to because
it might interfere

02:21:42.256 --> 02:21:43.486
with the sound system.

02:21:48.389 --> 02:21:49.180
So what do we have?

02:21:49.180 --> 02:21:51.590
We have 2,500 graduating today?

02:21:51.590 --> 02:21:53.384
SEAMANS: 2,500
diplomas given out.

02:21:53.384 --> 02:21:57.650
There's actually-- a lot
of those are duplicate.

02:21:57.650 --> 02:22:00.020
By that, a person gets
two degrees at once.

02:22:00.020 --> 02:22:01.790
KEYSER: Because
there's a lot of--

02:22:01.790 --> 02:22:03.320
right, dual majors.

02:22:03.320 --> 02:22:07.630
SEAMANS: And there's about 1,000
from the undergraduate school.

02:22:07.630 --> 02:22:11.680
And probably about 1,200
from the graduate school,

02:22:11.680 --> 02:22:12.930
individuals that receive them.

02:22:12.930 --> 02:22:13.310
KEYSER: Right.

02:22:13.310 --> 02:22:14.410
And then there's
2,200, 2,500 degrees.

02:22:14.410 --> 02:22:16.035
SEAMANS: And some of
the undergraduates

02:22:16.035 --> 02:22:17.770
will receive a master's
degree as well as

02:22:17.770 --> 02:22:19.510
an undergraduate degree.

02:22:19.510 --> 02:22:22.231
But it totals up to
about that amount.

02:22:22.231 --> 02:22:24.179
And that has remained con--

02:22:30.023 --> 02:22:30.990
Yeah, here they are.

02:22:30.990 --> 02:22:33.490
They're all keeping in touch
with one another, just in case.

02:22:33.490 --> 02:22:34.031
KEYSER: Yeah.

02:22:34.031 --> 02:22:36.860
You see those two guys with
their backs to one another.

02:22:36.860 --> 02:22:39.970
They're actually
talking to one another.

02:22:39.970 --> 02:22:41.350
You see, they--

02:22:41.350 --> 02:22:42.657
SEAMANS: Uh-oh.

02:22:42.657 --> 02:22:43.240
KEYSER: Uh-oh.

02:22:43.240 --> 02:22:48.741
Yeah, they've--
now they're waving.

02:22:48.741 --> 02:22:49.240
Yeah, look.

02:22:49.240 --> 02:22:50.864
He's telling the guy
next to him, look.

02:22:50.864 --> 02:22:51.808
Look at the camera.

02:22:51.808 --> 02:22:53.752
That's the new generation.

02:22:58.010 --> 02:22:58.760
Yes, that's right.

02:22:58.760 --> 02:23:00.980
You're either talking to
the world over a cell phone

02:23:00.980 --> 02:23:02.896
or looking at it through
the lens of a camera.

02:23:02.896 --> 02:23:05.201
SEAMANS: Yes.

02:23:05.201 --> 02:23:08.090
KEYSER: I mean, I
suppose I can't really

02:23:08.090 --> 02:23:10.505
take a superior position,
since I wear glasses.

02:23:19.040 --> 02:23:21.300
She's tired, and we
haven't even started yet.

02:23:21.300 --> 02:23:24.389
There's a female
graduate of the--

02:23:24.389 --> 02:23:25.335
SEAMANS: Navy ROTC.

02:23:29.705 --> 02:23:31.830
KEYSER: I do wish they'd
be a little more inventive

02:23:31.830 --> 02:23:33.960
with their mortarboards.

02:23:33.960 --> 02:23:36.825
SEAMANS: Yeah, that phase
seems to have passed.

02:23:42.010 --> 02:23:44.385
KEYSER: The color of the--

02:23:44.385 --> 02:23:45.890
you've probably
noticed-- there is

02:23:45.890 --> 02:23:49.955
Rafael Bras on the right, the
incoming Chair of the Faculty.

02:23:49.955 --> 02:23:54.200
And you can see he has a
rather unusual and ornate

02:23:54.200 --> 02:23:55.070
academic costume.

02:23:55.070 --> 02:23:57.870
I believe that's from
the University of Toledo,

02:23:57.870 --> 02:23:59.299
is that right, in Spain.

02:23:59.299 --> 02:23:59.840
SEAMANS: Yes.

02:23:59.840 --> 02:24:04.730
KEYSER: And you can see it
has a bib and gold embroidery.

02:24:04.730 --> 02:24:07.260
You'll notice that the
way it works in academia

02:24:07.260 --> 02:24:09.920
is the higher up you get, the
more ridiculous the costume.

02:24:13.095 --> 02:24:14.720
SEAMANS: Well, I
guess you have to have

02:24:14.720 --> 02:24:17.350
something that embarrasses you,
reaching such a high level.

02:24:17.350 --> 02:24:19.100
KEYSER: But you know
what was really good?

02:24:19.100 --> 02:24:21.410
Yesterday, after the
hooding ceremony,

02:24:21.410 --> 02:24:25.660
I walked into the
coffee shop in Lobby 7.

02:24:25.660 --> 02:24:28.310
And the woman who was serving
me, her name was Carrie,

02:24:28.310 --> 02:24:33.872
she had a hat on which looked
exactly like the hat I had on.

02:24:33.872 --> 02:24:36.380
So I asked her if I could
come behind the counter

02:24:36.380 --> 02:24:37.580
and help her--

02:24:37.580 --> 02:24:39.040
SEAMANS: Get a good paying job?

02:24:39.040 --> 02:24:40.581
KEYSER: She welcomed
me behind there.

02:24:40.581 --> 02:24:43.265
I went behind the counter and
served a couple of customers.

02:24:50.400 --> 02:24:53.360
Well, I always
kept my mortarboard

02:24:53.360 --> 02:24:56.900
on throughout these ceremonies
because I knew that there'd

02:24:56.900 --> 02:24:58.350
be camera shots like this.

02:24:58.350 --> 02:25:00.529
And I'm losing a lot of my hair.

02:25:00.529 --> 02:25:03.070
SEAMANS: Well, you don't have
to worry about that under here,

02:25:03.070 --> 02:25:04.510
because nobody can really see--

02:25:04.510 --> 02:25:05.843
KEYSER: Nobody's looking, right.

02:25:05.843 --> 02:25:07.610
That's the great
thing about this job.

02:25:07.610 --> 02:25:08.390
Nobody can see us.

02:25:08.390 --> 02:25:10.220
Now, there's Suzanne
Flynn, the Department

02:25:10.220 --> 02:25:12.885
of Linguistics and Philosophy,
who's straightening her hair.

02:25:12.885 --> 02:25:14.260
SEAMANS: Without
her mortarboard.

02:25:14.260 --> 02:25:17.110
KEYSER: In between those two
august-looking gentlemen.

02:25:17.110 --> 02:25:21.280
And next to her on the
left is Pete Donaldson,

02:25:21.280 --> 02:25:26.321
who has done some wonderful work
in putting Shakespeare onto--

02:25:26.321 --> 02:25:27.320
SEAMANS: DVD and other--

02:25:27.320 --> 02:25:29.092
KEYSER: --DVDs.

02:25:29.092 --> 02:25:33.140
And there's Phil
Clay, the upper right,

02:25:33.140 --> 02:25:35.030
with his hand on his chin.

02:25:35.030 --> 02:25:36.500
And he's the Chancellor.

02:25:36.500 --> 02:25:39.721
And talking to him is Bob
Brown, who's the MIT Provost.

02:25:39.721 --> 02:25:41.220
SEAMANS: The chief
academic officer.

02:25:41.220 --> 02:25:42.678
KEYSER: The chief
academic officer.

02:25:42.678 --> 02:25:45.320
And the two of those are
really the people who

02:25:45.320 --> 02:25:47.270
report directly to Chuck Vest.

02:25:47.270 --> 02:25:51.080
And there, to the right, is the
outgoing Chair of the Faculty.

02:25:51.080 --> 02:25:52.780
That's Steve Graves, I believe.

02:25:52.780 --> 02:25:57.020
And in front is Paul Legase,
who is Chair of the Commencement

02:25:57.020 --> 02:26:01.064
Committee, and one of the chief
organizers of this event, one

02:26:01.064 --> 02:26:03.030
of the chief shepherds.

02:26:03.030 --> 02:26:06.688
And next to him is
Eleanor Westney,

02:26:06.688 --> 02:26:09.807
who is an expert on Japanese--

02:26:09.807 --> 02:26:10.640
SEAMANS: Technology.

02:26:10.640 --> 02:26:11.134
KEYSER: --industry.

02:26:11.134 --> 02:26:11.628
SEAMANS: Industry.

02:26:11.628 --> 02:26:13.211
KEYSER: And she's
in the Sloan School.

02:26:16.549 --> 02:26:18.340
SEAMANS: Well, we don't
have that blue sky,

02:26:18.340 --> 02:26:21.370
but at least we
don't have sheets

02:26:21.370 --> 02:26:24.220
of rain coming down
as we did last year

02:26:24.220 --> 02:26:26.140
at this particular juncture.

02:26:26.140 --> 02:26:28.730
KEYSER: You really
have to have experience

02:26:28.730 --> 02:26:31.806
of a whole host of
these under your belt

02:26:31.806 --> 02:26:33.970
to know how nice it is a day.

02:26:33.970 --> 02:26:36.700
I remember one time that
it was pouring rain,

02:26:36.700 --> 02:26:39.190
and there was a
20-knot wind, and I

02:26:39.190 --> 02:26:40.630
thought that the
sail was actually

02:26:40.630 --> 02:26:43.240
going to take off and carry
us all up into the steps.

02:26:43.240 --> 02:26:44.920
SEAMANS: From the
opposite extreme,

02:26:44.920 --> 02:26:50.400
in 1930, when Carl Compton was
inaugurated in the Great Court,

02:26:50.400 --> 02:26:54.314
as it was known at that time,
it was over 100 degrees in the--

02:26:54.314 --> 02:26:55.480
KEYSER: Well, that's brutal.

02:26:55.480 --> 02:26:58.880
SEAMANS: Because people just
were fainting it was so hot.

02:26:58.880 --> 02:27:01.780
In fact, the only people who
stayed out were the faculty.

02:27:01.780 --> 02:27:06.151
Everybody else was lined up
against the side of the--

02:27:06.151 --> 02:27:08.939
in the shade of the
buildings and of the trees.

02:27:08.939 --> 02:27:10.480
KEYSER: Well, there's
Larry Benedict.

02:27:10.480 --> 02:27:12.147
SEAMANS: The mace is
being [INAUDIBLE]..

02:27:12.147 --> 02:27:14.482
KEYSER: [INAUDIBLE] is
carrying the mace up now, yep.

02:27:18.220 --> 02:27:21.490
SEAMANS: Again,
the symbol of MIT,

02:27:21.490 --> 02:27:28.890
transferring its degree-giving
power to the students.

02:27:28.890 --> 02:27:30.084
There's the seal again.

02:27:39.930 --> 02:27:42.990
The seal, which you will
have lots of views of it

02:27:42.990 --> 02:27:45.390
during the ceremony,
will indicate--

02:27:45.390 --> 02:27:49.300
you have two figures on
either side of an anvil.

02:27:49.300 --> 02:27:53.720
And these are, again,
the mind and the hand,

02:27:53.720 --> 02:27:54.955
which are part of the--

02:27:54.955 --> 02:27:56.580
KEYSER: We're going
to say goodbye now.

02:27:56.580 --> 02:27:56.835
The program is about to start.

02:27:56.835 --> 02:27:59.050
D'ARBELOFF: The Corporation
and the faculty--

02:27:59.050 --> 02:27:59.220
So so long everyone.

02:27:59.220 --> 02:28:01.110
D'ARBELOFF: --of the
Massachusetts Institute

02:28:01.110 --> 02:28:01.320
of Technology--

02:28:01.320 --> 02:28:01.370
KEYSER: This is Jay Keyser.

02:28:01.370 --> 02:28:02.536
SEAMANS: And Warren Seamans.

02:28:02.536 --> 02:28:05.040
D'ARBELOFF: --are now
declared convened.

02:28:05.040 --> 02:28:09.180
Together with this
assembly on the occasion

02:28:09.180 --> 02:28:13.370
of the commencement
exercises of this Institution

02:28:13.370 --> 02:28:16.280
for the conferring
of its degrees.

02:28:16.280 --> 02:28:19.470
The stage assembly
and audience will

02:28:19.470 --> 02:28:25.440
please rise for the invocation
by Reverend Paul Reynolds,

02:28:25.440 --> 02:28:28.860
and remain standing
to join the MIT

02:28:28.860 --> 02:28:32.460
Chorallaries in the
singing of one verse

02:28:32.460 --> 02:28:34.023
of "The Star-Spangled Banner."

02:28:42.681 --> 02:28:47.200
REYNOLDS: Shalom, salaam, peace.

02:28:47.200 --> 02:28:52.530
Creator of life, bless us as
we gather in your presence.

02:28:52.530 --> 02:28:55.230
Your spirit of wisdom
fills the earth,

02:28:55.230 --> 02:28:57.930
reminding us that we
are sisters and brothers

02:28:57.930 --> 02:29:01.110
in a single human family.

02:29:01.110 --> 02:29:03.780
Pour out your blessings
on these women and men,

02:29:03.780 --> 02:29:08.250
graduates of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology.

02:29:08.250 --> 02:29:10.650
Sustain the passion
of their intelligence,

02:29:10.650 --> 02:29:14.040
and nurture in them
a restless curiosity,

02:29:14.040 --> 02:29:18.300
which marvels at the intricacy
of the universe and revels

02:29:18.300 --> 02:29:21.000
in its beauty and wonder.

02:29:21.000 --> 02:29:23.250
Sharpen the focus
of their insights

02:29:23.250 --> 02:29:25.710
and temper the generosity
of their hearts

02:29:25.710 --> 02:29:30.180
with deepening compassion
for themselves and others,

02:29:30.180 --> 02:29:33.630
that they may always
carry a thirst for truth,

02:29:33.630 --> 02:29:39.270
a longing for justice, and
an abiding sense of hope.

02:29:39.270 --> 02:29:41.780
Enclose them in the
circle of your care,

02:29:41.780 --> 02:29:46.230
and create in them the courage
of honest conviction, that you

02:29:46.230 --> 02:29:50.280
may use the work of their
hands in the service

02:29:50.280 --> 02:29:53.460
of the family of nations.

02:29:53.460 --> 02:29:56.400
Pour out your joy on
their family and friends

02:29:56.400 --> 02:29:59.700
during these festive
days of celebration,

02:29:59.700 --> 02:30:03.480
and nurture always the
life of this Institute

02:30:03.480 --> 02:30:09.000
and its community of research,
scholarship, and service.

02:30:09.000 --> 02:30:13.200
May our graduates go forth
into the world and their future

02:30:13.200 --> 02:30:17.710
with a passion for
knowledge and wisdom.

02:30:17.710 --> 02:30:21.370
May they go forth into the
world and illumine the universe

02:30:21.370 --> 02:30:24.310
with hope and light.

02:30:24.310 --> 02:30:28.450
And with our well-wishes to
accompany them on their way,

02:30:28.450 --> 02:30:33.310
may they set the world on
fire with love for peace

02:30:33.310 --> 02:30:35.650
and for justice.

02:30:35.650 --> 02:30:40.510
Shalom, salaam, peace.

02:30:40.510 --> 02:30:46.095
Creator of life, bless us as
we gather in your presence.

02:30:46.095 --> 02:30:46.595
Amen.

02:30:51.565 --> 02:30:56.038
[MUSIC - "THE STAR SPANGLED
 BANNER"]

02:31:02.499 --> 02:31:08.381
CHORALLARIES: (SINGING)
O, say can you see,

02:31:08.381 --> 02:31:15.400
by the dawn's early
light, what so proudly

02:31:15.400 --> 02:31:22.510
we hailed at the twilight's
last gleaming, whose

02:31:22.510 --> 02:31:31.980
broad stripes and bright stars
through the perilous fight,

02:31:31.980 --> 02:31:41.114
o'er the ramparts we watched
were so gallantly streaming?

02:31:41.114 --> 02:31:49.050
And the rockets' red glare,
the bombs bursting in air

02:31:49.050 --> 02:31:58.970
gave proof through the night
that our flag was still there.

02:31:58.970 --> 02:32:10.874
O, say does that star-spangled
banner yet wave o'er

02:32:10.874 --> 02:32:22.282
the land of the free and
the home of the brave?

02:32:26.250 --> 02:32:29.722
[APPLAUSE]

02:32:34.186 --> 02:32:35.674
D'ARBELOFF: Be seated.

02:32:38.250 --> 02:32:43.350
I am pleased to welcome to the
platform George J. Mitchell,

02:32:43.350 --> 02:32:46.380
former United States
Senator from Maine,

02:32:46.380 --> 02:32:50.040
and the honorable Michael
A. Sullivan, Mayor

02:32:50.040 --> 02:32:52.350
of the City of Cambridge.

02:32:52.350 --> 02:32:55.744
Senator Mitchell will
now give the address.

02:32:59.656 --> 02:33:02.590
[APPLAUSE]

02:33:07.969 --> 02:33:12.850
MITCHELL: To the graduates,
their families and friends,

02:33:12.850 --> 02:33:17.230
it's an honor for me to be
part of this important day

02:33:17.230 --> 02:33:19.220
in your lives.

02:33:19.220 --> 02:33:23.060
I know that many of you already
are worrying about how long I'm

02:33:23.060 --> 02:33:25.860
going to speak.

02:33:25.860 --> 02:33:29.930
So I begin by reassuring
you that my intention is

02:33:29.930 --> 02:33:35.430
to stop speaking before you
graduates stop listening.

02:33:35.430 --> 02:33:37.410
I've been asked
to discuss my work

02:33:37.410 --> 02:33:41.490
in conflict resolution in
Northern Ireland and the Middle

02:33:41.490 --> 02:33:42.890
East.

02:33:42.890 --> 02:33:45.930
Mindful of the commitment
I've just made,

02:33:45.930 --> 02:33:50.110
my comments will
have to be brief.

02:33:50.110 --> 02:33:55.170
I spent five years working
for peace in Northern Ireland.

02:33:55.170 --> 02:34:01.100
For almost all of that
time, there was no progress.

02:34:01.100 --> 02:34:06.260
There was political
posturing, delay, doubt,

02:34:06.260 --> 02:34:08.660
and many people died.

02:34:08.660 --> 02:34:12.890
After each death, I
spent a sleepless night

02:34:12.890 --> 02:34:19.130
trying to fight off a growing
feeling of despair and failure.

02:34:19.130 --> 02:34:22.310
But after years of
effort, a peace agreement

02:34:22.310 --> 02:34:24.080
was finally reached.

02:34:24.080 --> 02:34:28.130
From that experience,
I formed the conviction

02:34:28.130 --> 02:34:33.170
that there is no such thing as
a conflict that can't be ended.

02:34:33.170 --> 02:34:36.170
Conflicts are
created, conducted,

02:34:36.170 --> 02:34:38.600
and sustained by human beings.

02:34:38.600 --> 02:34:41.990
They can be ended
by human beings.

02:34:41.990 --> 02:34:44.630
After completing my work
in Northern Ireland,

02:34:44.630 --> 02:34:47.030
I was asked to go
to the Middle East.

02:34:47.030 --> 02:34:50.210
There, the committee
which I chaired

02:34:50.210 --> 02:34:53.600
made recommendations which
have been incorporated

02:34:53.600 --> 02:34:56.990
into the roadmap,
recently advanced

02:34:56.990 --> 02:35:00.470
by the United States, the
United Nations, the European

02:35:00.470 --> 02:35:02.480
Union, and Russia.

02:35:02.480 --> 02:35:08.270
As we all know, last week
it was accepted by Israel

02:35:08.270 --> 02:35:11.210
and the Palestinian Authority.

02:35:11.210 --> 02:35:17.030
Although only a first step,
that acceptance was significant.

02:35:17.030 --> 02:35:21.500
And it reinforces my conviction
that like all others,

02:35:21.500 --> 02:35:25.310
the conflict in the
Middle East can be ended.

02:35:25.310 --> 02:35:27.920
Among the steps we
recommended and which

02:35:27.920 --> 02:35:33.350
are included in the roadmap are
by the Palestinian Authority

02:35:33.350 --> 02:35:39.650
the renunciation of violence,
an end to hatred and incitement,

02:35:39.650 --> 02:35:45.770
and a 100% effort to crack down
on terrorism, an effort which

02:35:45.770 --> 02:35:47.990
has not yet been made.

02:35:47.990 --> 02:35:51.770
By the government of Israel,
the withdrawal of its military

02:35:51.770 --> 02:35:55.970
forces to their
pre-intifada positions,

02:35:55.970 --> 02:35:59.420
and the freezing of all
settlement construction

02:35:59.420 --> 02:36:00.920
activity.

02:36:00.920 --> 02:36:03.590
Then there must be
a prompt resumption

02:36:03.590 --> 02:36:08.700
of negotiations to achieve
a two-state solution.

02:36:08.700 --> 02:36:12.630
What is most important
is that both recognize

02:36:12.630 --> 02:36:16.710
that the only way to
achieve a lasting solution

02:36:16.710 --> 02:36:18.870
is through negotiation.

02:36:18.870 --> 02:36:22.410
If they are to
succeed, illusions

02:36:22.410 --> 02:36:26.200
must be abandoned on both sides.

02:36:26.200 --> 02:36:29.070
Some Palestinians
and other Arabs

02:36:29.070 --> 02:36:30.840
continue to believe
that they can

02:36:30.840 --> 02:36:36.150
destroy Israel and rid the
Middle East of a Jewish state.

02:36:36.150 --> 02:36:39.460
That cannot and will not happen.

02:36:39.460 --> 02:36:43.920
It is a fantasy that will
only generate more misery

02:36:43.920 --> 02:36:47.200
and suffering on both sides.

02:36:47.200 --> 02:36:50.340
Some Israelis
believe and advocate

02:36:50.340 --> 02:36:52.590
that all of the Palestinians--

02:36:52.590 --> 02:36:55.020
every man, woman, and child--

02:36:55.020 --> 02:36:59.850
can be physically uprooted
and moved to another country.

02:36:59.850 --> 02:37:03.040
That cannot and will not happen.

02:37:03.040 --> 02:37:06.000
It, too, is a fantasy.

02:37:06.000 --> 02:37:08.130
Contributing to the
difficulty of finding

02:37:08.130 --> 02:37:10.020
a peaceful resolution
is the fact

02:37:10.020 --> 02:37:13.290
that the circumstances and
objectives of the two sides

02:37:13.290 --> 02:37:14.700
are different.

02:37:14.700 --> 02:37:16.680
The Israelis have a state.

02:37:16.680 --> 02:37:19.200
What they want is security.

02:37:19.200 --> 02:37:21.840
That is their
overriding objective.

02:37:21.840 --> 02:37:26.270
The Palestinians don't have
a state, and they want one--

02:37:26.270 --> 02:37:30.570
an independent, economically
viable, geographically

02:37:30.570 --> 02:37:32.370
contiguous state.

02:37:32.370 --> 02:37:35.530
That is their
overriding objective.

02:37:35.530 --> 02:37:40.180
I believe that neither
can attain its objective

02:37:40.180 --> 02:37:43.990
by denying to the other
side its objective.

02:37:43.990 --> 02:37:47.050
Palestinians will
never achieve a state

02:37:47.050 --> 02:37:50.080
if Israel does
not have security.

02:37:50.080 --> 02:37:53.680
Israel will never get
sustainable security

02:37:53.680 --> 02:37:57.530
if the Palestinians
don't have a state.

02:37:57.530 --> 02:37:59.360
There are some in
both societies--

02:37:59.360 --> 02:38:00.950
perhaps some here today--

02:38:00.950 --> 02:38:03.320
who disagree with
this assertion.

02:38:03.320 --> 02:38:06.050
But for me, it
has been validated

02:38:06.050 --> 02:38:10.370
by the tragic events of the
past two and a half years.

02:38:10.370 --> 02:38:14.750
Our committee's report was
very tough on terrorism.

02:38:14.750 --> 02:38:18.050
We branded it "morally
reprehensible and

02:38:18.050 --> 02:38:19.800
unacceptable."

02:38:19.800 --> 02:38:23.360
It is also politically
counterproductive.

02:38:23.360 --> 02:38:26.120
It will not achieve
its objective.

02:38:26.120 --> 02:38:30.170
To the contrary, with
each suicide bomb attack,

02:38:30.170 --> 02:38:34.490
the prospect of a
Palestinian state is delayed.

02:38:34.490 --> 02:38:36.950
Such tactics also
are destructive

02:38:36.950 --> 02:38:40.940
of Palestinian civil
society and the reputation

02:38:40.940 --> 02:38:44.430
of the Palestinian people
throughout the world.

02:38:44.430 --> 02:38:48.140
The roadmap offers
Palestinians the alternative

02:38:48.140 --> 02:38:51.980
of a nonviolent path
to a Jewish state--

02:38:51.980 --> 02:38:56.930
to a Palestinian state living in
peace alongside a Jewish state,

02:38:56.930 --> 02:39:01.310
the two-state solution that
a majority on both sides

02:39:01.310 --> 02:39:05.160
continue to say they support.

02:39:05.160 --> 02:39:08.000
Palestinians, in
turn, must accept

02:39:08.000 --> 02:39:09.990
that the Israeli
demand for security

02:39:09.990 --> 02:39:15.420
is as real and as necessary as
is their demand for a state.

02:39:15.420 --> 02:39:20.070
Both are more likely to occur
if reciprocal steps are taken

02:39:20.070 --> 02:39:24.300
to create a context in which
meaningful negotiation can

02:39:24.300 --> 02:39:26.090
be conducted.

02:39:26.090 --> 02:39:29.840
This can't be done by
the two sides themselves.

02:39:29.840 --> 02:39:32.990
Their mutual mistrust is total.

02:39:32.990 --> 02:39:35.930
The culture of peace,
so carefully nurtured

02:39:35.930 --> 02:39:39.620
over the previous decade,
has been shattered.

02:39:39.620 --> 02:39:41.330
In its place,
there has developed

02:39:41.330 --> 02:39:46.730
a sense of futility and
despair of the inevitability

02:39:46.730 --> 02:39:48.350
of conflict.

02:39:48.350 --> 02:39:53.090
And yet public opinion
polls on both sides

02:39:53.090 --> 02:39:56.240
show consistent and
strong majorities

02:39:56.240 --> 02:39:59.270
in support of a
two-state solution

02:39:59.270 --> 02:40:03.920
and of the political process
needed to bring it about.

02:40:03.920 --> 02:40:07.550
But because of the impact
of the prolonged violence

02:40:07.550 --> 02:40:11.450
a majority of Palestinians
support suicide bombings

02:40:11.450 --> 02:40:14.870
of Israelis, and a
majority of Israelis

02:40:14.870 --> 02:40:17.080
support the use
of whatever force

02:40:17.080 --> 02:40:21.320
is deemed necessary to
suppress such attacks.

02:40:21.320 --> 02:40:25.250
In other words,
majorities on both sides

02:40:25.250 --> 02:40:29.510
largely agree on the solution,
but they no longer trust

02:40:29.510 --> 02:40:32.480
the other side's
intentions to reach it.

02:40:32.480 --> 02:40:35.810
They're caught in
a zero-sum contest

02:40:35.810 --> 02:40:39.360
in which both are suffering.

02:40:39.360 --> 02:40:42.450
Time does not
permit me to repeat

02:40:42.450 --> 02:40:45.780
the long list of our
committee's recommendations

02:40:45.780 --> 02:40:48.030
or the details of the roadmap.

02:40:48.030 --> 02:40:51.480
Most attention has been given
to the cessation of violence

02:40:51.480 --> 02:40:55.710
and incitement by Palestinians
and a freeze on all settlement

02:40:55.710 --> 02:40:59.250
construction
activity by Israelis.

02:40:59.250 --> 02:41:04.260
I have already commented on and
condemned the failure of terror

02:41:04.260 --> 02:41:06.750
to advance the
Palestinian cause.

02:41:06.750 --> 02:41:09.570
As to settlements,
our committee adopted

02:41:09.570 --> 02:41:13.650
what has been the policy of
every American administration

02:41:13.650 --> 02:41:17.420
for more than a
quarter of a century.

02:41:17.420 --> 02:41:19.740
During the more than 50
years of its existence,

02:41:19.740 --> 02:41:23.130
Israel has had the strong
support of the United States.

02:41:23.130 --> 02:41:26.250
In international forums,
the US has at times

02:41:26.250 --> 02:41:30.360
cast the only vote
in Israel's behalf.

02:41:30.360 --> 02:41:33.700
Yet even in such a
close relationship,

02:41:33.700 --> 02:41:35.580
there are some differences.

02:41:35.580 --> 02:41:38.890
Prominent among them is the
United States government's

02:41:38.890 --> 02:41:41.850
long-standing opposition
to the government

02:41:41.850 --> 02:41:45.210
of Israel's policies
and practices regarding

02:41:45.210 --> 02:41:46.260
settlements.

02:41:46.260 --> 02:41:49.590
That US opposition
has been consistent

02:41:49.590 --> 02:41:53.820
through the Carter, Reagan,
Bush, Clinton, and Bush

02:41:53.820 --> 02:41:55.290
administrations.

02:41:55.290 --> 02:41:59.820
Just as consistent has been the
continued settlement activity

02:41:59.820 --> 02:42:02.310
by the Israeli government.

02:42:02.310 --> 02:42:04.500
It his major policy
speech on the Middle East

02:42:04.500 --> 02:42:07.890
last year, President
Bush was explicit.

02:42:07.890 --> 02:42:11.760
He said, and I quote,
"Israeli settlement activity

02:42:11.760 --> 02:42:15.810
in the occupied territories
must stop, consistent

02:42:15.810 --> 02:42:18.930
with the recommendations
of the Mitchell committee."

02:42:18.930 --> 02:42:22.080
The roadmap also
refers specifically

02:42:22.080 --> 02:42:25.780
to our recommendations
on settlements.

02:42:25.780 --> 02:42:28.120
As we read in this
morning's newspaper,

02:42:28.120 --> 02:42:30.910
there already are
serious problems

02:42:30.910 --> 02:42:34.690
in implementation of the
roadmap's recommendations.

02:42:34.690 --> 02:42:36.370
There will be more.

02:42:36.370 --> 02:42:39.310
So I don't underestimate
the difficulties.

02:42:39.310 --> 02:42:43.630
But I believe that an end to
this conflict is possible.

02:42:43.630 --> 02:42:48.730
It is especially important
that we Americans not turn away

02:42:48.730 --> 02:42:51.220
when the inevitable
setbacks occur,

02:42:51.220 --> 02:42:55.720
that we not resign ourselves to
the inevitability of conflict.

02:42:55.720 --> 02:42:59.170
American commitment
and determination

02:42:59.170 --> 02:43:02.110
are essential to
the preservation

02:43:02.110 --> 02:43:06.280
of a sovereign and secure Israel
and to the peaceful and just

02:43:06.280 --> 02:43:10.450
resolution of the conflict
in the Middle East.

02:43:10.450 --> 02:43:16.330
There, as elsewhere, there
is a universal human desire

02:43:16.330 --> 02:43:21.550
to lead lives that are
full, free, and meaningful.

02:43:21.550 --> 02:43:23.560
Because of the
wisdom and foresight

02:43:23.560 --> 02:43:26.830
of our founding fathers,
we're fortunate to live

02:43:26.830 --> 02:43:30.970
in a society which
despite its imperfections

02:43:30.970 --> 02:43:36.670
is the most free, the most just,
the most open society in all

02:43:36.670 --> 02:43:38.410
of human history.

02:43:38.410 --> 02:43:43.420
From that society, each of
us receives many benefits.

02:43:43.420 --> 02:43:47.770
With benefits come
responsibilities.

02:43:47.770 --> 02:43:52.660
You've had the good fortune to
receive an advanced education,

02:43:52.660 --> 02:43:56.620
so you have an important
role to play in preserving

02:43:56.620 --> 02:43:59.500
and improving our way of life.

02:43:59.500 --> 02:44:02.800
There is much for
all of us to do.

02:44:02.800 --> 02:44:07.810
Each of you students,
graduates, undergraduates,

02:44:07.810 --> 02:44:10.870
every person present
today, will have

02:44:10.870 --> 02:44:15.040
your own list of our
society's domestic challenges.

02:44:15.040 --> 02:44:19.420
I will mention just a few
that are important to me.

02:44:19.420 --> 02:44:24.130
If you believe, as I do,
that every American child is

02:44:24.130 --> 02:44:28.240
entitled to a good education,
regardless of background

02:44:28.240 --> 02:44:31.300
or family wealth,
you must oppose

02:44:31.300 --> 02:44:34.180
any actions which have
the effect of denying

02:44:34.180 --> 02:44:36.730
them that opportunity.

02:44:36.730 --> 02:44:40.360
If you believe, as I do,
that we have an obligation

02:44:40.360 --> 02:44:44.200
to leave for future
generations the very basics

02:44:44.200 --> 02:44:46.660
of healthy human life--

02:44:46.660 --> 02:44:51.490
clean air, pure water,
unpoisoned land--

02:44:51.490 --> 02:44:56.860
you must demand public policies
to honor that obligation.

02:44:56.860 --> 02:45:00.910
If you believe, as I do, that
every American is entitled

02:45:00.910 --> 02:45:04.960
to equal opportunity
and equal justice,

02:45:04.960 --> 02:45:10.060
you must speak out against
all forms of discrimination

02:45:10.060 --> 02:45:11.770
and injustice.

02:45:11.770 --> 02:45:15.790
Never forget that in
the presence of evil,

02:45:15.790 --> 02:45:19.570
silence makes you an accomplice.

02:45:19.570 --> 02:45:23.440
The education you have received
that this great institution is

02:45:23.440 --> 02:45:27.610
important, even
necessary, but it is not

02:45:27.610 --> 02:45:29.830
a guarantee of self-worth.

02:45:29.830 --> 02:45:33.400
It is not a substitute
for a life of effort.

02:45:33.400 --> 02:45:36.670
How you do it is important--

02:45:36.670 --> 02:45:40.210
just as important
as what you do.

02:45:40.210 --> 02:45:44.170
If you take pride in what
you do, you will excel.

02:45:44.170 --> 02:45:49.330
If you do not take pride in
what you do, you cannot excel.

02:45:49.330 --> 02:45:54.940
John Gardner put it best when he
wrote, "An excellent plumber is

02:45:54.940 --> 02:45:59.920
infinitely more admirable than
an incompetent philosopher.

02:45:59.920 --> 02:46:03.820
The society which scorns
excellence in plumbing,

02:46:03.820 --> 02:46:07.150
because it regards that
as a humble activity,

02:46:07.150 --> 02:46:10.300
and tolerates shoddiness
in philosophy,

02:46:10.300 --> 02:46:13.230
because it is an
exalted activity,

02:46:13.230 --> 02:46:17.110
will have neither good
plumbing nor good philosophy.

02:46:17.110 --> 02:46:20.620
Neither its pipes nor its
theories will hold water."

02:46:27.880 --> 02:46:31.760
For those undergraduates
going on to further education,

02:46:31.760 --> 02:46:35.330
I have some advice
on what not to do.

02:46:35.330 --> 02:46:37.850
I serve as the
Chancellor of the Queen's

02:46:37.850 --> 02:46:40.310
University of Northern Ireland.

02:46:40.310 --> 02:46:44.270
Last December, our students
took a graduate school entrance

02:46:44.270 --> 02:46:45.290
exam.

02:46:45.290 --> 02:46:49.490
One of them, frustrated by the
difficulty of the questions,

02:46:49.490 --> 02:46:52.790
wrote, in answer to
the last question,

02:46:52.790 --> 02:46:56.510
"God alone knows the
answer to this question.

02:46:56.510 --> 02:46:58.970
Merry Christmas."

02:46:58.970 --> 02:47:02.405
Two weeks later, he got his
paper back with this note.

02:47:02.405 --> 02:47:05.420
"God gets an A. You fail.

02:47:05.420 --> 02:47:06.200
Happy new year."

02:47:10.160 --> 02:47:14.060
You are part of a highly
privileged elite--

02:47:14.060 --> 02:47:17.840
in the education you
received, in the opportunities

02:47:17.840 --> 02:47:20.270
that will be available to you.

02:47:20.270 --> 02:47:23.480
It is inevitable and
appropriate that you

02:47:23.480 --> 02:47:26.060
devote much of your
life to earning

02:47:26.060 --> 02:47:31.760
the income you need to support
yourself and your family.

02:47:31.760 --> 02:47:36.860
Like all human beings, you
will want status and wealth,

02:47:36.860 --> 02:47:39.110
and most of you will get them.

02:47:39.110 --> 02:47:42.710
But the more successful
you are, the more evident

02:47:42.710 --> 02:47:47.480
it will become to you that
there is more to life.

02:47:47.480 --> 02:47:50.540
You will find that
fulfillment in your life

02:47:50.540 --> 02:47:53.930
will not come from the
expensive things you

02:47:53.930 --> 02:47:59.510
acquire, not from leisure,
not from self-indulgence.

02:47:59.510 --> 02:48:01.670
Real fulfillment
in life will come

02:48:01.670 --> 02:48:07.100
from striving with all of your
physical and spiritual might

02:48:07.100 --> 02:48:12.230
for a worthwhile objective
that helps others and is larger

02:48:12.230 --> 02:48:14.330
than your self-interest.

02:48:14.330 --> 02:48:17.900
Most of all for you graduates,
I hope that each of you

02:48:17.900 --> 02:48:22.770
is fortunate enough to find
such an objective in your life.

02:48:22.770 --> 02:48:23.770
Congratulations.

02:48:23.770 --> 02:48:24.750
Good luck.

02:48:24.750 --> 02:48:27.690
May God bless each one of you.

02:48:27.690 --> 02:48:31.610
[APPLAUSE]

02:48:44.840 --> 02:48:47.080
D'ARBELOFF: Thank
you, Senator Mitchell.

02:48:47.080 --> 02:48:50.990
These are words that give
us hope for the future.

02:48:50.990 --> 02:48:54.310
And we all hope that the
parties in the Middle East

02:48:54.310 --> 02:48:58.940
follow the Mitchell Report
and the resulting roadmap.

02:48:58.940 --> 02:49:03.010
And also, you've given the
graduates a wonderful roadmap

02:49:03.010 --> 02:49:04.130
for their future.

02:49:04.130 --> 02:49:05.440
Thank you.

02:49:05.440 --> 02:49:10.390
Now, Mr. Suneth Wijesinghe,
President of the Graduate

02:49:10.390 --> 02:49:13.960
Student Council, will
give a salute to MIT

02:49:13.960 --> 02:49:16.330
from the graduate student body.

02:49:16.330 --> 02:49:20.830
Following this, Mr.
Sina Kevin Nazemi,

02:49:20.830 --> 02:49:24.910
President of the Senior Class,
will present the class gift

02:49:24.910 --> 02:49:28.848
to President Vest, after which
the President will deliver

02:49:28.848 --> 02:49:30.800
his charge to the graduates.

02:49:44.952 --> 02:49:48.960
WIJESINGHE: Fellow
colleagues, Sigmund Freud,

02:49:48.960 --> 02:49:52.100
the great medical
doctor and psychologist,

02:49:52.100 --> 02:49:56.840
is quoted as saying,
"One day, in retrospect,

02:49:56.840 --> 02:49:58.940
the years of struggle
will strike you

02:49:58.940 --> 02:50:01.450
as the most beautiful."

02:50:01.450 --> 02:50:04.770
Indeed, as we gather here
today in Killian Court

02:50:04.770 --> 02:50:08.940
to celebrate our accomplishment
on becoming MIT's newest

02:50:08.940 --> 02:50:12.580
ambassadors to the nation
and the world at large,

02:50:12.580 --> 02:50:16.530
it is a time to reflect on those
memories and experiences that

02:50:16.530 --> 02:50:19.860
have shaped our
journey here at MIT.

02:50:19.860 --> 02:50:22.590
Now, you might ask
yourself what hope there

02:50:22.590 --> 02:50:25.440
is to find beauty in struggle.

02:50:25.440 --> 02:50:28.380
Ultimately, this will depend
on your particular definition

02:50:28.380 --> 02:50:29.370
of beauty.

02:50:29.370 --> 02:50:32.580
However the writer and
educator Walter Pater

02:50:32.580 --> 02:50:34.590
gives us some guidelines.

02:50:34.590 --> 02:50:37.200
"Beauty, like all
other qualities

02:50:37.200 --> 02:50:40.680
presented to human
experience, is relative.

02:50:40.680 --> 02:50:44.250
And the definition of it
becomes unmeaning and useless

02:50:44.250 --> 02:50:47.240
in proportion to
its abstractness.

02:50:47.240 --> 02:50:50.520
To define beauty not
in the most abstract

02:50:50.520 --> 02:50:53.070
but in the most concrete
terms possible, not

02:50:53.070 --> 02:50:55.620
to find a universal
formula for it

02:50:55.620 --> 02:50:58.950
but the formula which
expresses most adequately

02:50:58.950 --> 02:51:02.250
this or that special
manifestation of it,

02:51:02.250 --> 02:51:05.700
is the aim of the true
student of aesthetics."

02:51:05.700 --> 02:51:09.030
Hence, in order to remember
those special manifestations

02:51:09.030 --> 02:51:13.080
or expressions of beauty, I ask
that you take your mind back

02:51:13.080 --> 02:51:17.280
to the start of your MIT journey
and remember the excitement you

02:51:17.280 --> 02:51:20.370
felt on the day you received
the letter announcing

02:51:20.370 --> 02:51:24.210
your admission, the first
days at orientation,

02:51:24.210 --> 02:51:27.750
settling into a new home
and academic department,

02:51:27.750 --> 02:51:30.540
your first class
and problems sets,

02:51:30.540 --> 02:51:34.110
tailored to a very unique
style of MIT education,

02:51:34.110 --> 02:51:36.900
one that teaches you how
to learn and to think,

02:51:36.900 --> 02:51:40.350
and the challenging research
and teaching you undertook where

02:51:40.350 --> 02:51:44.430
you were given opportunities
to make your mark on the world.

02:51:44.430 --> 02:51:47.850
There are bound to be very
personal expressions of beauty

02:51:47.850 --> 02:51:51.510
captured here in a landscape
of ideas, relationships,

02:51:51.510 --> 02:51:53.430
and emotions.

02:51:53.430 --> 02:51:55.830
While the specifics
of our experiences

02:51:55.830 --> 02:51:59.160
may be quite varied, there
are many common threads

02:51:59.160 --> 02:52:03.270
that do, however, bind
our collective endeavors.

02:52:03.270 --> 02:52:07.770
On the surface, this consists
of a shared sense of place,

02:52:07.770 --> 02:52:10.890
defined by our living
and working spaces,

02:52:10.890 --> 02:52:13.200
a shared passage
of time, captured

02:52:13.200 --> 02:52:16.620
by the pace and intensity
of our daily chores,

02:52:16.620 --> 02:52:20.250
and perhaps the most profound,
a shared sense of attraction

02:52:20.250 --> 02:52:21.630
to events with free food.

02:52:24.550 --> 02:52:26.760
But beyond this
is a common thread

02:52:26.760 --> 02:52:31.300
that I believe is especially
unique to graduates of MIT.

02:52:31.300 --> 02:52:34.020
This is a quality that allows
us to seamlessly relate

02:52:34.020 --> 02:52:38.430
to one another when we meet in
five, 10, 15, or even 50 years

02:52:38.430 --> 02:52:40.110
from now.

02:52:40.110 --> 02:52:42.450
This is a quality
of unbounded passion

02:52:42.450 --> 02:52:45.000
that we bring to bear
on any activity that

02:52:45.000 --> 02:52:48.600
captures our hearts, our
minds, and our souls.

02:52:48.600 --> 02:52:51.270
It is this passion that
crystallizes solutions

02:52:51.270 --> 02:52:53.880
to serve fellow
citizens, that pushes

02:52:53.880 --> 02:52:57.530
the boundaries of research and
teaching excellence, and which

02:52:57.530 --> 02:53:02.040
fuels scientific, technological,
and business innovation.

02:53:02.040 --> 02:53:04.080
Passion has indeed
defined the journey

02:53:04.080 --> 02:53:08.280
we embarked on here at MIT and
is the very source of beauty

02:53:08.280 --> 02:53:11.590
in any endeavor of excellence.

02:53:11.590 --> 02:53:14.920
As you take on the role of an
MIT ambassador to the world,

02:53:14.920 --> 02:53:17.500
your demonstrated
passion for excellence

02:53:17.500 --> 02:53:22.090
will translate to a unique style
of leadership second to none.

02:53:22.090 --> 02:53:25.030
I charge you to motivate,
inspire, and assist

02:53:25.030 --> 02:53:27.280
those around you to
formulate and reach

02:53:27.280 --> 02:53:31.090
new goals through high standards
of professionalism, courtesy,

02:53:31.090 --> 02:53:33.010
and mutual respect.

02:53:33.010 --> 02:53:36.610
But more importantly, I charge
you to help realize solutions

02:53:36.610 --> 02:53:40.420
for a society that is seeking
to find new meaning and new role

02:53:40.420 --> 02:53:42.010
models.

02:53:42.010 --> 02:53:44.650
In closing, I encourage you
to maintain relationships

02:53:44.650 --> 02:53:46.450
with the multitude
of student groups

02:53:46.450 --> 02:53:48.790
that you may have been
involved with and benefited

02:53:48.790 --> 02:53:50.830
from over the years.

02:53:50.830 --> 02:53:53.170
By and large, we
cherish most experiences

02:53:53.170 --> 02:53:55.270
we share with our
friends and colleagues.

02:53:55.270 --> 02:53:58.150
And student groups undoubtedly
played the greatest part

02:53:58.150 --> 02:54:00.790
in helping to
create these bonds.

02:54:00.790 --> 02:54:02.410
I encourage you to
serve as a mentor

02:54:02.410 --> 02:54:04.680
to the new generation
of student leaders

02:54:04.680 --> 02:54:08.140
who will face many of the
same challenges that you did.

02:54:08.140 --> 02:54:09.940
With that, I'd like
to congratulate you

02:54:09.940 --> 02:54:13.060
all once again on reaching
the conclusion of one journey

02:54:13.060 --> 02:54:14.710
and the start of another.

02:54:14.710 --> 02:54:17.319
Here's wishing you the very
best of good fortune in all

02:54:17.319 --> 02:54:19.062
your future endeavors.

02:54:19.062 --> 02:54:23.046
[APPLAUSE]

02:54:36.990 --> 02:54:40.800
NAZEMI: My fellow graduates,
it's been quite a journey.

02:54:40.800 --> 02:54:47.590
Four years, 72 freshmen year
problem sets, 4,394 Anne Hunter

02:54:47.590 --> 02:54:53.780
emails, eight MIT Nobel
Prizes, 15 calls to mom and dad

02:54:53.780 --> 02:54:59.300
asking for more money,
30,000 sequenced human genes,

02:54:59.300 --> 02:55:06.300
45 hacks, 43 Sloan
C-Functions, one dot-com boom,

02:55:06.300 --> 02:55:12.620
one dot-com bust, and finally
a 786 smoot procession

02:55:12.620 --> 02:55:15.830
from Johnson Athletic
Center to Killian Court, we,

02:55:15.830 --> 02:55:23.145
the 2,202 graduates of the
MIT class of 2003 are here.

02:55:23.145 --> 02:55:26.596
[APPLAUSE]

02:55:29.570 --> 02:55:31.970
Giving the parting
words is difficult.

02:55:31.970 --> 02:55:35.330
We can leave obvious advice
for the next generation.

02:55:35.330 --> 02:55:37.880
If your GPA is
suffering, cross-register

02:55:37.880 --> 02:55:38.885
for classes at Harvard.

02:55:42.350 --> 02:55:45.530
But on a more serious
note, we have all

02:55:45.530 --> 02:55:48.830
been given the greatest gift
anyone could ever ask for--

02:55:48.830 --> 02:55:50.960
the gift of opportunity.

02:55:50.960 --> 02:55:54.230
We are all here because of the
sacrifices of our loved ones.

02:55:54.230 --> 02:55:57.230
For many of us, the sacrifices
involved our loved ones

02:55:57.230 --> 02:55:59.390
leaving their homelands
and their families

02:55:59.390 --> 02:56:02.540
in the name of creating
opportunities for us.

02:56:02.540 --> 02:56:05.360
While the stories of
sacrifice differ among us,

02:56:05.360 --> 02:56:07.640
graduation for all
of us represents

02:56:07.640 --> 02:56:11.900
not just our own work but
also that of our loved ones.

02:56:11.900 --> 02:56:15.412
Let us clearly say to them
on this day of celebration,

02:56:15.412 --> 02:56:16.380
thank you.

02:56:16.380 --> 02:56:19.768
[APPLAUSE]

02:56:25.590 --> 02:56:28.080
As the old adage
goes, much is expected

02:56:28.080 --> 02:56:31.170
from those to whom
much has been given.

02:56:31.170 --> 02:56:33.810
We have been blessed with a lot.

02:56:33.810 --> 02:56:37.260
It's time for us to give back.

02:56:37.260 --> 02:56:41.100
We can leave MIT merely
being reactive to the world's

02:56:41.100 --> 02:56:43.410
problems, considering
the world's problems

02:56:43.410 --> 02:56:45.180
in the abstract
and at a distance.

02:56:48.360 --> 02:56:51.150
But it is my hope that we leave
the world being active world

02:56:51.150 --> 02:56:53.050
citizens.

02:56:53.050 --> 02:56:55.210
The route of treating
the world's problems

02:56:55.210 --> 02:56:56.860
in the abstract
and at a distance

02:56:56.860 --> 02:57:00.880
will likely be comfortable, both
financially and emotionally,

02:57:00.880 --> 02:57:05.080
and we may very well be deemed
as extremely successful people.

02:57:05.080 --> 02:57:07.540
On the other hand, we
can leave MIT committing

02:57:07.540 --> 02:57:09.670
to actively engaging the
world's many and growing

02:57:09.670 --> 02:57:12.970
problems by saying
what needs to be said,

02:57:12.970 --> 02:57:15.300
doing what needs to be done.

02:57:15.300 --> 02:57:17.050
We can look at problems
like world poverty

02:57:17.050 --> 02:57:20.470
not as mere statistics
but as challenges--

02:57:20.470 --> 02:57:24.020
challenges we are
willing to face head on.

02:57:24.020 --> 02:57:27.680
My fellow classmates, the
commitment need not be uniform.

02:57:27.680 --> 02:57:29.870
I hope that each of us
finds it within ourselves

02:57:29.870 --> 02:57:33.380
to take a few moments at every
stage of the very busy lives

02:57:33.380 --> 02:57:36.620
that we are likely to lead
to reflect on where we can be

02:57:36.620 --> 02:57:39.500
active, on where our
words and our actions

02:57:39.500 --> 02:57:41.247
can have great impact.

02:57:41.247 --> 02:57:42.830
While making such a
commitment may not

02:57:42.830 --> 02:57:46.460
result in as comfortable a
life as merely being reactive,

02:57:46.460 --> 02:57:50.510
it has the collective
potential to be revolutionary.

02:57:50.510 --> 02:57:52.760
I'd like to end this morning
with a story once shared

02:57:52.760 --> 02:57:55.240
with me about a boy named Jimmy.

02:57:55.240 --> 02:57:57.170
On one of the few
days this school year

02:57:57.170 --> 02:57:59.450
when the sun was
shining in Cambridge,

02:57:59.450 --> 02:58:02.129
Jimmy grabbed his
baseball and glove

02:58:02.129 --> 02:58:04.670
and approached his father, who
was sitting on the living room

02:58:04.670 --> 02:58:09.020
couch, reading his copy of MIT's
Technology Review magazine.

02:58:09.020 --> 02:58:12.080
Dad, let's go play
catch, Jimmy begged.

02:58:12.080 --> 02:58:13.010
Not now, son.

02:58:13.010 --> 02:58:14.390
I'm reading a magazine.

02:58:14.390 --> 02:58:16.910
Well, Jimmy went away
for about two minutes.

02:58:16.910 --> 02:58:18.980
And he came back
and he said, Dad,

02:58:18.980 --> 02:58:20.890
can we go out and
play catch now?

02:58:20.890 --> 02:58:22.170
You said later.

02:58:22.170 --> 02:58:23.600
It's later.

02:58:23.600 --> 02:58:26.480
Father, trying to occupy Jimmy's
time so he could get back

02:58:26.480 --> 02:58:29.030
to reading his magazine,
flipped through the magazine

02:58:29.030 --> 02:58:32.420
to a page with a picture of
the world from space on it.

02:58:32.420 --> 02:58:35.930
And he tore the page out, and
he ripped it into small pieces.

02:58:35.930 --> 02:58:39.560
He said, son, Jimmy, when you
put the pieces of the world

02:58:39.560 --> 02:58:42.560
together, we will go
out and play catch.

02:58:42.560 --> 02:58:45.110
Well, the father went back
to reading his magazine,

02:58:45.110 --> 02:58:48.620
and to his surprise, Jimmy came
back just two minutes later

02:58:48.620 --> 02:58:51.350
with the pieces of the
world all taped together.

02:58:51.350 --> 02:58:52.560
The father was shocked.

02:58:52.560 --> 02:58:56.070
He said, son, Jimmy,
how did you do that?

02:58:56.070 --> 02:58:58.430
How did you put the world
together so quickly?

02:58:58.430 --> 02:59:00.350
The world is so complicated.

02:59:00.350 --> 02:59:02.810
Jimmy smiled, and
he held up the page

02:59:02.810 --> 02:59:04.435
with the picture
of the world on it,

02:59:04.435 --> 02:59:05.810
and he flipped it
over and showed

02:59:05.810 --> 02:59:09.320
his father that on the other
side was a picture of a man.

02:59:09.320 --> 02:59:12.290
He said, Dad, it was easy.

02:59:12.290 --> 02:59:18.732
I put the man together, and the
world came together by itself.

02:59:18.732 --> 02:59:22.564
[APPLAUSE]

02:59:25.450 --> 02:59:28.820
My fellow classmates,
today is our day.

02:59:28.820 --> 02:59:31.690
And shortly this
will be our world.

02:59:31.690 --> 02:59:33.610
Let us better
ourselves and the world

02:59:33.610 --> 02:59:36.700
we inherit by putting
ourselves together

02:59:36.700 --> 02:59:38.806
as active world citizens.

02:59:38.806 --> 02:59:39.794
Thank you.

02:59:47.220 --> 02:59:48.930
I now have the
privilege of taking part

02:59:48.930 --> 02:59:52.568
in two MIT traditions, the
first being the turning

02:59:52.568 --> 02:59:55.058
of our MIT ring, the Brass Rat.

02:59:59.540 --> 03:00:04.010
I now ask all graduates to
join me in taking off your ring

03:00:04.010 --> 03:00:07.520
and turning the beaver around
to officially let the world know

03:00:07.520 --> 03:00:09.690
that you are an MIT graduate.

03:00:09.690 --> 03:00:11.650
Congratulations.

03:00:11.650 --> 03:00:14.100
[APPLAUSE]

03:00:17.050 --> 03:00:19.450
I would now like to invite
President Vest to join me

03:00:19.450 --> 03:00:20.980
on the stage for
the presentation

03:00:20.980 --> 03:00:23.670
of the senior gift.

03:00:23.670 --> 03:00:28.070
Dr. Vest, I'm proud to announce
that 25% of the members

03:00:28.070 --> 03:00:31.130
of the Class of 2003 have
contributed to our senior gift,

03:00:31.130 --> 03:00:37.370
for a total of $28,187.

03:00:37.370 --> 03:00:40.262
[APPLAUSE]

03:00:42.690 --> 03:00:44.350
Part of this money
will be used to fund

03:00:44.350 --> 03:00:46.560
a seal of the Institute,
which will be placed

03:00:46.560 --> 03:00:49.800
in the main lobby
of MIT, Lobby 7,

03:00:49.800 --> 03:00:53.460
as well as a scholarship
fund for future MIT students.

03:00:53.460 --> 03:00:55.740
The amount of our gift
has been made possible

03:00:55.740 --> 03:00:58.830
by the President of the Alumni
Association, Jim Lash, who

03:00:58.830 --> 03:01:00.751
has progressively
matched our donations.

03:01:00.751 --> 03:01:02.715
Mr. Lash, thank you.

03:01:09.100 --> 03:01:13.216
Dr. Vest, on behalf of
the MIT class of 2003,

03:01:13.216 --> 03:01:17.997
I proudly present to
you our senior gift.

03:01:17.997 --> 03:01:20.955
[APPLAUSE]

03:01:24.406 --> 03:01:29.630
VEST: Thank you very much,
Mr. Nazemi and Mr. Wijesinghe.

03:01:29.630 --> 03:01:32.510
I am most grateful for
your words and your gifts

03:01:32.510 --> 03:01:36.380
but especially for all that
you and your fellow students

03:01:36.380 --> 03:01:39.980
have meant to this
great institution.

03:01:39.980 --> 03:01:43.010
At this time, I want
to take particular note

03:01:43.010 --> 03:01:44.930
of a group of
remarkable individuals

03:01:44.930 --> 03:01:49.130
here today, the members
of the Class of 1953.

03:01:49.130 --> 03:01:53.576
You can recognize them
by their red jackets--

03:01:53.576 --> 03:01:56.540
[APPLAUSE]

03:01:59.010 --> 03:02:01.410
--by their red jackets
and, I suspect,

03:02:01.410 --> 03:02:05.590
by a certain aura of experience
they carry with them.

03:02:05.590 --> 03:02:09.179
The world has changed greatly
since their own graduation.

03:02:09.179 --> 03:02:10.720
And with all that
they have witnessed

03:02:10.720 --> 03:02:13.480
in the intervening years,
I am sure that they

03:02:13.480 --> 03:02:17.640
could tell us much about what
is truly valuable in life.

03:02:17.640 --> 03:02:20.320
And they could also
tell us about one thing

03:02:20.320 --> 03:02:22.060
that never changes--

03:02:22.060 --> 03:02:24.400
the pride you take
in knowing that you

03:02:24.400 --> 03:02:27.550
have earned a degree from MIT.

03:02:27.550 --> 03:02:30.940
To the Class of 1953, welcome.

03:02:30.940 --> 03:02:33.940
[APPLAUSE]

03:02:37.940 --> 03:02:42.140
So here we are, gathered
once again in Killian Court

03:02:42.140 --> 03:02:46.960
to celebrate accomplishment,
heritage, and passage.

03:02:46.960 --> 03:02:49.630
It may perhaps seem
odd that a community so

03:02:49.630 --> 03:02:53.080
dedicated to the future
comes together donning

03:02:53.080 --> 03:02:56.790
strange and colorful
medieval regalia.

03:02:56.790 --> 03:02:58.920
But indeed, it is
fitting and seemingly

03:02:58.920 --> 03:03:04.900
fulfilling of a deep human need
that such rituals take place.

03:03:04.900 --> 03:03:08.990
One of our MIT poets,
Professor Stephen Tapscott

03:03:08.990 --> 03:03:12.260
has reminded us, however,
that the solemnity

03:03:12.260 --> 03:03:14.480
of such elegant
scenes is sometimes

03:03:14.480 --> 03:03:19.850
broken by the faculty themselves
when, as he once wrote,

03:03:19.850 --> 03:03:23.030
"From deep in the drooping
sleeves of their robes,

03:03:23.030 --> 03:03:26.780
surreptitiously they bring
out peanut butter sandwiches

03:03:26.780 --> 03:03:30.470
to sustain them during
the long ceremony."

03:03:30.470 --> 03:03:34.250
Be that as it may, this ritual
reminds us of the continuity

03:03:34.250 --> 03:03:38.900
through the ages of our role in
an unbroken centuries-old chain

03:03:38.900 --> 03:03:41.480
of discovery and accomplishment.

03:03:41.480 --> 03:03:44.300
But above all it celebrates
your accomplishments

03:03:44.300 --> 03:03:46.890
during your student years.

03:03:46.890 --> 03:03:49.040
This is not to say that
you have accomplished

03:03:49.040 --> 03:03:51.230
this remarkable
feat of graduating

03:03:51.230 --> 03:03:53.750
from MIT on your own.

03:03:53.750 --> 03:03:57.880
We are surrounded by parents,
family, spouses, and children,

03:03:57.880 --> 03:04:01.780
who have supported and sustained
you through these years.

03:04:01.780 --> 03:04:06.010
You will recognize them today
by their smiles, brought

03:04:06.010 --> 03:04:08.920
about by their great
relief, by their great pride

03:04:08.920 --> 03:04:11.590
in your accomplishment,
and no doubt

03:04:11.590 --> 03:04:15.320
by a sense of great relief
to their bank accounts.

03:04:15.320 --> 03:04:18.070
We are very grateful
that they are all here.

03:04:18.070 --> 03:04:20.710
And it is also
especially wonderful

03:04:20.710 --> 03:04:23.230
to see the babies and
small children who

03:04:23.230 --> 03:04:26.740
come to see their mothers and
fathers, sisters and brothers,

03:04:26.740 --> 03:04:28.180
graduate.

03:04:28.180 --> 03:04:29.800
They too are welcome.

03:04:29.800 --> 03:04:32.530
And as this ceremony
stretches onward,

03:04:32.530 --> 03:04:35.380
I give them special
presidential approval

03:04:35.380 --> 03:04:40.120
to comment upon the proceedings
at any time and in any manner

03:04:40.120 --> 03:04:42.500
they see fit.

03:04:42.500 --> 03:04:46.260
And now some thoughts
for our students.

03:04:46.260 --> 03:04:49.120
You are soon to become graduates
of one of the world's most

03:04:49.120 --> 03:04:51.490
prestigious universities.

03:04:51.490 --> 03:04:54.910
We celebrate your journey
to this graduation,

03:04:54.910 --> 03:04:56.770
but more importantly,
we look forward

03:04:56.770 --> 03:04:59.260
to the continuation
of your journey

03:04:59.260 --> 03:05:02.980
as you dedicate your talents
and scholarship to the service

03:05:02.980 --> 03:05:06.050
of your nations and the world.

03:05:06.050 --> 03:05:10.370
The world we share today is
one of enormous complexity.

03:05:10.370 --> 03:05:12.320
At the dawn of this
new century, we

03:05:12.320 --> 03:05:16.000
have unprecedented
capabilities and opportunities

03:05:16.000 --> 03:05:19.730
in science and engineering
to explore and improve

03:05:19.730 --> 03:05:21.940
the human condition.

03:05:21.940 --> 03:05:25.900
Indeed, this is the most
exciting period in science

03:05:25.900 --> 03:05:29.900
and its applications
in all of history.

03:05:29.900 --> 03:05:33.500
We have before us the
ability to come ever closer

03:05:33.500 --> 03:05:36.500
to understanding the
origins of our universe

03:05:36.500 --> 03:05:38.560
and the life within it.

03:05:38.560 --> 03:05:41.400
We can learn the evasive
facts of dark energy

03:05:41.400 --> 03:05:45.090
and dark matter that
may pervade our cosmos.

03:05:45.090 --> 03:05:47.250
We are accelerating
our understanding

03:05:47.250 --> 03:05:50.130
of the human brain and mind.

03:05:50.130 --> 03:05:53.070
We have mapped the human
genome, and now begins

03:05:53.070 --> 03:05:56.280
the task of relating this
knowledge to the origins

03:05:56.280 --> 03:05:59.140
and resolution of disease.

03:05:59.140 --> 03:06:01.560
We have the ability to
improve human learning

03:06:01.560 --> 03:06:03.780
and communication.

03:06:03.780 --> 03:06:08.040
We can now build materials and
devices at the molecular level

03:06:08.040 --> 03:06:12.390
and can mimic more closely the
beautiful and efficient designs

03:06:12.390 --> 03:06:14.470
found in nature.

03:06:14.470 --> 03:06:19.080
We can dramatically improve
our use of energy and materials

03:06:19.080 --> 03:06:23.010
and learn to better steward
our Earth's environment.

03:06:23.010 --> 03:06:28.130
We can learn to feed and shelter
the peoples of our planet.

03:06:28.130 --> 03:06:32.700
Behind the advances in each
of these areas of knowledge

03:06:32.700 --> 03:06:36.750
and of challenge lies
some common features.

03:06:36.750 --> 03:06:41.160
They all require work at the
highest levels of complexity.

03:06:41.160 --> 03:06:43.980
They all cross
traditional boundaries--

03:06:43.980 --> 03:06:46.290
boundaries between
academic disciplines,

03:06:46.290 --> 03:06:49.260
between academia,
industry, and government,

03:06:49.260 --> 03:06:53.070
and between nations and
peoples around the globe.

03:06:53.070 --> 03:06:55.920
Many of these advances
originate in the ability

03:06:55.920 --> 03:07:00.630
to think more holistically,
think more in terms of systems,

03:07:00.630 --> 03:07:03.810
to combine and integrate
as well as to apply

03:07:03.810 --> 03:07:06.910
reductionist concepts.

03:07:06.910 --> 03:07:08.870
The advance of
knowledge increasingly

03:07:08.870 --> 03:07:12.710
comes from combining and
pulling together rather than

03:07:12.710 --> 03:07:16.750
breaking down into finer
and finer elements.

03:07:16.750 --> 03:07:19.280
As graduates of MIT,
you of all people

03:07:19.280 --> 03:07:22.040
stand ready to
drive these advances

03:07:22.040 --> 03:07:24.590
and lead their
wise applications.

03:07:24.590 --> 03:07:28.350
You can make a better future.

03:07:28.350 --> 03:07:30.540
Gerard O'Neill once
discussed the work

03:07:30.540 --> 03:07:32.730
of those who had
made serious attempts

03:07:32.730 --> 03:07:37.290
at various stages of history
to predict the future.

03:07:37.290 --> 03:07:41.580
He analyzed whether those
predictions had come true.

03:07:41.580 --> 03:07:44.050
What he learned was
that we invariably

03:07:44.050 --> 03:07:48.150
underestimate the rate of
scientific and technological

03:07:48.150 --> 03:07:52.980
progress, and we invariably
and dramatically overestimate

03:07:52.980 --> 03:07:55.770
the rate of social progress.

03:07:55.770 --> 03:07:59.370
So you should strive to
change this situation,

03:07:59.370 --> 03:08:04.130
to bring better harmony
to technology and society.

03:08:04.130 --> 03:08:06.800
As you work to advance
scientific and engineering

03:08:06.800 --> 03:08:10.310
knowledge or to apply
it, you must increasingly

03:08:10.310 --> 03:08:16.190
understand the social,
political, economic, artistic,

03:08:16.190 --> 03:08:20.060
and historical contexts
in which you work.

03:08:20.060 --> 03:08:22.520
And when you do so, I
think that in these realms,

03:08:22.520 --> 03:08:26.880
you too will find
a familiar tension.

03:08:26.880 --> 03:08:31.080
Because our social and political
world also is subject to two

03:08:31.080 --> 03:08:34.480
opposing forces.

03:08:34.480 --> 03:08:37.810
One force is that
of fragmentation,

03:08:37.810 --> 03:08:40.680
pulling us apart.

03:08:40.680 --> 03:08:44.420
The other force is that of
integration and connection,

03:08:44.420 --> 03:08:47.110
bringing us together.

03:08:47.110 --> 03:08:53.170
We are increasingly fragmented
by politics, poverty, hatred,

03:08:53.170 --> 03:08:56.990
absolutism, and fear.

03:08:56.990 --> 03:09:01.080
Yet we are pulled together
by information technology,

03:09:01.080 --> 03:09:05.560
by the challenges of the Earth's
environment that we all share,

03:09:05.560 --> 03:09:10.210
by the globalization of trade,
economies, and education,

03:09:10.210 --> 03:09:13.030
and by hope.

03:09:13.030 --> 03:09:16.660
Fragmentation stems from
our worst tendencies.

03:09:16.660 --> 03:09:21.450
Integration stems from the
best of our shared aspirations.

03:09:21.450 --> 03:09:23.820
And today we have
many new opportunities

03:09:23.820 --> 03:09:27.420
to apply our skills in
science and engineering

03:09:27.420 --> 03:09:33.340
in a way that advances these
shared human aspirations.

03:09:33.340 --> 03:09:37.010
I have spoken of the
opportunities before you.

03:09:37.010 --> 03:09:39.830
But as has just been said,
with great opportunities

03:09:39.830 --> 03:09:42.790
comes great responsibilities.

03:09:42.790 --> 03:09:44.710
As you leave MIT,
I hope that you

03:09:44.710 --> 03:09:47.080
will take with you more
than just the knowledge

03:09:47.080 --> 03:09:49.140
you have gained.

03:09:49.140 --> 03:09:51.630
Take with you the
sense of wonder and joy

03:09:51.630 --> 03:09:56.430
that drives science and
that science instills in us.

03:09:56.430 --> 03:09:58.740
Recognize the
promise that is held

03:09:58.740 --> 03:10:02.880
in the exploration of nature
and the quest for new knowledge

03:10:02.880 --> 03:10:05.290
and new understanding.

03:10:05.290 --> 03:10:08.560
And recognize the value of
the intellectual heritage

03:10:08.560 --> 03:10:11.040
on which you build.

03:10:11.040 --> 03:10:14.700
But your task is to
shape the future.

03:10:14.700 --> 03:10:17.510
This will require more than
the knowledge and skills

03:10:17.510 --> 03:10:20.130
that you have gained here.

03:10:20.130 --> 03:10:24.030
The philosopher Alfred
North Whitehead once said,

03:10:24.030 --> 03:10:28.030
it is the business of the
future to be dangerous.

03:10:28.030 --> 03:10:31.650
And so you will need to be more
than smart and knowledgeable.

03:10:31.650 --> 03:10:34.290
You will need to be courageous.

03:10:34.290 --> 03:10:38.620
You will need to be creative
and compassionate as well.

03:10:38.620 --> 03:10:43.510
We expect no less of you, and
indeed, we are counting on you.

03:10:43.510 --> 03:10:46.900
I urge you to help us build
a world community that

03:10:46.900 --> 03:10:50.980
embraces and values different
cultures and heritage that

03:10:50.980 --> 03:10:54.340
respects individuals and
works toward the betterment

03:10:54.340 --> 03:10:55.900
of all people.

03:10:55.900 --> 03:10:58.900
There is fulfillment
in such service.

03:10:58.900 --> 03:11:01.720
In the words of
Albert Schweitzer,

03:11:01.720 --> 03:11:04.120
"I don't know what
your destiny will be.

03:11:04.120 --> 03:11:06.310
But one thing I know--

03:11:06.310 --> 03:11:09.760
the ones among you who
will be really happy

03:11:09.760 --> 03:11:14.930
are those who have sought
and found how to serve."

03:11:14.930 --> 03:11:18.020
As graduates of MIT, you
are in a unique position

03:11:18.020 --> 03:11:20.180
to serve and to lead.

03:11:20.180 --> 03:11:22.810
You've acquired an
outstanding education.

03:11:22.810 --> 03:11:24.260
It has not been a gift.

03:11:24.260 --> 03:11:27.010
You have worked for
it, and worked hard.

03:11:27.010 --> 03:11:31.890
Nonetheless, your education
has been a privilege.

03:11:31.890 --> 03:11:35.670
Now you have a responsibility
to use your education,

03:11:35.670 --> 03:11:40.420
to use it wisely,
and use it well.

03:11:40.420 --> 03:11:44.620
Today, we celebrate with joy
your journey to this graduation

03:11:44.620 --> 03:11:48.490
ceremony and look forward
with hope and optimism

03:11:48.490 --> 03:11:51.390
to your journey into the future.

03:11:51.390 --> 03:11:54.890
But as we do so, I would
like to share with you

03:11:54.890 --> 03:11:59.270
a brief personal reflection,
because I had an experience

03:11:59.270 --> 03:12:03.440
this spring that brought
this issue of fragmentation

03:12:03.440 --> 03:12:09.000
versus integration home to
me in a very personal way.

03:12:09.000 --> 03:12:11.610
I was invited to give
the commencement address

03:12:11.610 --> 03:12:14.390
at the University of Tokyo.

03:12:14.390 --> 03:12:17.050
Now, to put this
in perspective, you

03:12:17.050 --> 03:12:20.500
need to know that when I
was a young boy growing up

03:12:20.500 --> 03:12:23.170
in a small town
in West Virginia,

03:12:23.170 --> 03:12:28.560
the United States and Japan
were at war with each other.

03:12:28.560 --> 03:12:32.230
We were separated by
cultural, historical,

03:12:32.230 --> 03:12:35.310
and political differences
vastly greater

03:12:35.310 --> 03:12:39.010
than the geographic
distance that separated us.

03:12:39.010 --> 03:12:42.400
At that time, it would have
been inconceivable to me

03:12:42.400 --> 03:12:46.480
that I might someday
serve as President of MIT.

03:12:46.480 --> 03:12:50.260
And it would have been
even far more inconceivable

03:12:50.260 --> 03:12:54.820
that I might someday stand to
address the graduating class

03:12:54.820 --> 03:12:57.310
of the University of Tokyo.

03:12:57.310 --> 03:13:00.760
Yet there I was,
having crossed a bridge

03:13:00.760 --> 03:13:04.110
made possible by the
conviction of leaders

03:13:04.110 --> 03:13:07.130
and of ordinary citizens
in both countries

03:13:07.130 --> 03:13:11.920
that we had more in common
than we had differences.

03:13:11.920 --> 03:13:15.550
And while I was halfway around
the globe from Cambridge,

03:13:15.550 --> 03:13:17.770
I was speaking at
a university that

03:13:17.770 --> 03:13:20.780
has much in common with MIT--

03:13:20.780 --> 03:13:23.770
a commitment to
advance human knowledge

03:13:23.770 --> 03:13:26.800
and to work toward a world in
which all people can develop

03:13:26.800 --> 03:13:31.060
their talents, economies,
health, security, freedom,

03:13:31.060 --> 03:13:32.800
and opportunities.

03:13:32.800 --> 03:13:35.350
What better ambition
could there be

03:13:35.350 --> 03:13:41.780
for any institution or
individual in our world today?

03:13:41.780 --> 03:13:45.430
And now as you embark on the
next stage of your journey,

03:13:45.430 --> 03:13:51.100
I offer this charge to
you, the graduates of MIT.

03:13:51.100 --> 03:13:56.440
Ponder the unthinkable,
question the status quo,

03:13:56.440 --> 03:14:00.980
live in the world as well
as in your own nation, dream

03:14:00.980 --> 03:14:05.200
of a better future, but
contribute to the present,

03:14:05.200 --> 03:14:09.860
share your talents,
commune with all people,

03:14:09.860 --> 03:14:13.530
be steady friends
and bold companions,

03:14:13.530 --> 03:14:17.400
address the truly important
issues of your time,

03:14:17.400 --> 03:14:20.620
be honest in all that you do.

03:14:20.620 --> 03:14:23.965
Take your education your
talent and your energy,

03:14:23.965 --> 03:14:27.280
and build a nation and
a world community that

03:14:27.280 --> 03:14:31.040
considers knowledge
a gift to be shared,

03:14:31.040 --> 03:14:34.700
a healthy planet, a
place to be cherished,

03:14:34.700 --> 03:14:37.640
and human dignity
and opportunity

03:14:37.640 --> 03:14:43.200
fundamental conditions to
be enjoyed by all people.

03:14:43.200 --> 03:14:48.525
Men and women of MIT, I wish
you godspeed and the very

03:14:48.525 --> 03:14:50.950
best of good fortune.

03:14:50.950 --> 03:14:54.345
